def inland_on_decisions_admission(tid, *, stage="post", _iter_events, events_path=None,
                                  repo_root=None, decisions_dir=None,
                                  commit_reachable=None) -> dict:
    """SPEC-0204 rule 3's ADMISSION, asked from INSIDE `land` — T-12320.

    Returns `{"admitted": bool, "ceiling_ref": str|None, "refusal": {...}|None,
    "residual_count": int, "decisions_applied": int}`.

    WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. A `--rebaseline` land merges main and SPEC-0077 §3a then demands an
    audit-post over the MERGED tree — the T-11483 in-land churn re-audit. That re-audit was invoked
    as an ORDINARY `audit post`, so a card whose stage sits AT or PAST the SPEC-0124 ceiling had it
    refused by the ceiling and the land aborted, with no in-band way forward: the only exit was a
    Controller-driven `sync` -> `audit post --on-decisions --reaudit-after-close` -> `land` chain run
    tight enough that main did not move in between — a race against every other land, paid in auditor
    passes (T-12261 ran it twice on 2026-09-10; three `deviation_captured` rows, fingerprint
    `in-land-churn-reaudit-revokes-green-strands-held-branch-at-ceiling`). This asks the SAME question
    the CLI arm asks, so the land can take the ONE bounded pass the decisions already authorize.

    IT IS ONE MORE CALLER OF THE EXISTING LADDER, NOT A SECOND ONE (CHARTER §P5). Every step below is
    the chain `cmd_audit`'s `--on-decisions` arm walks, in its order: `_decide_journal_view` (the
    segment-aware view, SPEC-0190 rule 4) -> the `_on_decisions_row_is_real` PHANTOM filter (T-12310 —
    a pre-T-12309 carry-forward row wears `basis: on-decisions` without an auditor ever having run,
    and letting one rank as the ceiling row would bind decisions to a pass that did not happen) ->
    `_ceiling_row_of` -> `on_decisions_residuals` -> `on_decisions_admission`. Nothing here decides
    anything the ladder decides; this function only GATHERS.

    `reaudit_after_close=True` IS A FACT OF THE ROUTE, NEVER A CONVENIENCE. `cli.py#_inland_audit_post`
    invokes `audit post --reaudit-after-close`, whose own contract makes HEAD the subject for ANY
    decision set and therefore disables BOTH of the ladder's revision arms. So `ceiling_subject_revision`
    and `current_subject` are structurally unreachable inputs on this route and are passed None rather
    than guessed — a guessed value would be compared by an arm that is switched off, which is worse
    than absent because it reads as if it had been checked.

    THE REFUSAL KIND IS THE LADDER'S OWN, NEVER RE-LABELLED (audit-pre pass-1 finding
    fp1:760f51b0c9ce7c0b). Three sources, three kinds:

      - THE LADDER'S OWN refusal — `residual-undecided` (including its UNRESOLVABLE cause) or
        `evidence-revision-split` — is returned VERBATIM: kind, why, message and every diagnostic key
        it names. Re-labelling an unresolvable ceiling row `below-ceiling` would tell an operator who
        is ABOVE the ceiling that they are below it, and point at the wrong remedy.
      - `below-ceiling` ONLY for the two genuine PASS-COUNT readings, the same two `cmd_audit` refuses
        on: `no_completion_row` (no row for this (task, stage) exists, so there is no ceiling row) and
        `ceiling_row_passes_unresolved` (neither the row nor its saved record states a `passes`
        counter, so `ceiling_ref` cannot NAME a pass and no decision could ever have been bound to it).
      - `inland-admission-unreadable` for a read that RAISES. «I could not tell» is a third fact and
        must not borrow either kind above (`lessons/carving-an-exception-into-a-fail-closed-gate` §1).

    Fail-closed throughout and never raises: every path that cannot PROVE admission returns
    `admitted: False`. A caller cannot widen the gate through this function.

    Read-only: no event, no write, no auditor. It is the CALLER that decides whether to consult it at
    all — below the ceiling it is never called, which is what keeps that leg byte-identical."""
    try:
        view = _decide_journal_view(tid, stage, _iter_events=_iter_events, events_path=events_path)
        stage_rows = [r for r in view["stage_rows"]
                      if _on_decisions_row_is_real(r, view["stage_rows"], view["rows"], tid, stage)]
        # T-13561 — the saved record, read ONCE for both readers below: a counterless GREEN row
        # ranks by its own record's counter, and the residual read of the selected row reuses it.
        record = (prior_audit_record(tid, stage, decisions_dir=Path(decisions_dir))
                  if decisions_dir is not None else None)
        ceiling_row = _ceiling_row_of(stage_rows, record=record)
        if ceiling_row is None:
            return {"admitted": False, "ceiling_ref": None, "residual_count": 0,
                    "decisions_applied": 0,
                    "refusal": {
                        "kind": "below-ceiling", "why": "no_completion_row",
                        "message": (f"{tid} audit-{stage} has no `external_audit_completed` row in "
                                    f"the journal — there is no ceiling row whose residuals could be "
                                    f"decided (SPEC-0204 rule 3)."),
                    }}
        res = on_decisions_residuals(ceiling_row, stage_rows, tid, stage,
                                     repo_root=repo_root, decisions_dir=decisions_dir,
                                     record=record, commit_reachable=commit_reachable)
        passes = res.get("passes")
        if passes is None:
            # `ceiling_ref` IS `pass-<N>` read off that counter, and C1 warns that no caller may treat
            # its `None` as a number — so a row stating none cannot be NAMED, and nothing could have
            # been bound to it.
            return {"admitted": False, "ceiling_ref": None, "residual_count": 0,
                    "decisions_applied": 0,
                    "refusal": {
                        "kind": "below-ceiling", "why": "ceiling_row_passes_unresolved",
                        "message": (f"{tid} audit-{stage}: the ceiling row records no `passes` "
                                    f"counter and its saved record states none either, so "
                                    f"`ceiling_ref` cannot name a pass — the decisions could not be "
                                    f"bound to it (SPEC-0204 rules 2-3)."),
                    }}
        ceiling_ref = f"{tid}/{stage}/pass-{passes}"
        residual_keys = list(res.get("keys") or ())
        decisions = view["decisions"]
        bound = on_decisions_bind(decisions, ceiling_ref)
        _named, refusal = on_decisions_admission(
            tid=tid, stage=stage,
            residual_keys=residual_keys,
            decisions=decisions,
            ceiling_ref=ceiling_ref,
            ceiling_subject_revision=None,   # unreachable on this route — see the docstring
            current_subject=None,            # ditto
            reaudit_after_close=True,        # the in-land re-audit IS that route, by construction
            unresolvable=bool(res.get("unresolvable")),
            # T-12370 — CARRY C1's REASON, which this wrapper previously dropped while the CLI arm
            # passed it. It names WHICH check refused the record, and that is the difference between
            # «go find the record» and «the record you have is a DIFFERENT pass's» — the operator who
            # hit the live T-12315 case had to diff two YAMLs by hand to learn exactly that, from an
            # in-land refusal. No behaviour moves: the ladder appends it to the message it already
            # returns, and every admission decision is unchanged.
            unresolvable_reason=res.get("unresolvable_reason"),
            # Both revision arms are off on this route; the reader serves ONLY cause (ii)'s nested-set
            # derivation (T-13244), so a card whose `fix` decisions form a chain is not refused split
            # by the land's own §3a re-audit. Fail-closed as everywhere: unanswerable reads as a split.
            strict_descendant=(lambda _a, _b: _git_strict_descendant(
                _a, _b, repo_root=(repo_root[0] if isinstance(repo_root, (list, tuple)) and repo_root
                                   else repo_root))),
            # T-12542 — the closure binding is revision-independent, so it holds on this route too;
            # `subject_refusal` stays unset because this route audits HEAD, not a named revision.
            late_refs=late_finding_refs_by_fp(
                view["rows"], tid, stage, repo_root=repo_root,
                # T-13471 — the record's counter names the pass of a late finding no row counts.
                record_passes=(late_finding_record_passes(tid, decisions_dir=decisions_dir)
                               if decisions_dir is not None else None)),
        )
        return {"admitted": refusal is None, "ceiling_ref": ceiling_ref,
                "residual_count": len(residual_keys), "decisions_applied": len(bound),
                "refusal": refusal}
    except Exception as exc:      # noqa: BLE001 — a read that raises is its OWN third fact
        return {"admitted": False, "ceiling_ref": None, "residual_count": 0,
                "decisions_applied": 0,
                "refusal": {
                    "kind": "inland-admission-unreadable",
                    "why": "inland_admission_read_error",
                    "message": (f"{tid} audit-{stage}: the SPEC-0204 rule-3 admission could not be "
                                f"READ — {type(exc).__name__}: {exc}. Nothing is proven about "
                                f"whether the residuals are decided, so this pass is refused "
                                f"fail-closed (SPEC-0204 rule 3)."),
                }}


