def stage6_evidence_covers_subject(task_id, audited_commit, rows, resolve) -> bool:
    """T-12642 (SPEC-0204 rules 3-4) — does the engine's OWN Stage-6 ledger COVER the commit a
    terminal-bearing audit pass is about to audit? A PURE view over recorded rows; no I/O.

    IT TAKES NO FINDING, NO VERDICT AND NO SEVERITY, and that absence is the AC3 property, pinned
    mechanically by a signature assertion rather than promised in prose. The question is decided
    BEFORE any auditor is invoked, so at the moment of decision no auditor response exists — there
    is no prose, no `severity` and no `class_id` that could be read even by accident. The deciding
    input is the ledger and nothing else: this task's `tests_passed` rows (`ts`) and its
    `commit_landed` rows (`ts`, `data.commit`).

    WHY IT EXISTS. SPEC-0204 rule 3 makes a task audit stage TERMINAL the moment its `--on-decisions`
    pass returns RED. That irreversibility is correct for a substantive RED, and it was spent once
    (T-12622, 2026-09-16) on a residual that asserted only MISSING PROOF: the Stage-6 evidence did
    not postdate the audited commit. Proof can be supplied; a broken artifact cannot. So the engine
    refuses to SPEND a terminal-bearing pass while it can PROVE its own ledger does not cover the
    subject — no auditor runs, no verdict is recorded, no pass is spent, and therefore no terminality
    can arise. Terminality itself is untouched: nothing that reaches a verdict is spared, which is
    what keeps CHARTER §Principle 7's re-run-vs-resolve fork intact.

    TRUE iff EITHER disjunct holds:

      (A) CORRECTIVE COVERAGE — a `tests_passed` row for this task POSTDATES the audited commit's own
          `commit_landed` row. This is the strongest coverage there is (the run happened when the
          subject already existed) and it is exactly what the refusal message tells the session to
          produce, so it is what makes a refused pass admissible again. WITHOUT this disjunct the
          predicate would only ever look at runs PRECEDING the commit, would keep selecting the stale
          one, and the refusal would be UNESCAPABLE — a bounded correction route turned into a NEW
          dead end, which is the very defect this predicate exists to remove (audit-pre pass-2
          finding fp1:ba8feb4b0e6cb91d, TRUE and fixed here).

      (B) ORDINARY COVERAGE — the audited commit IS the FIRST `commit_landed` row for this task
          at-or-after the LATEST `tests_passed` row preceding it; i.e. the recorded run that precedes
          the subject was not ALREADY CONSUMED by an earlier commit of the same card. This is the
          normal Stage-6-then-Stage-7 flow, and it is what keeps every card admitted today admitted.
          The degenerate reading «the evidence must postdate the commit» was rejected because it is
          false of EVERY card: Stage 6 precedes Stage 7 by construction.

    MEASURED against the two real siblings that motivated the card. T-12621 ran 10:43:02Z and
    committed eca1206 at 10:43:39Z with NO intervening commit -> COVERED -> the pass is admitted and
    its substantive RED terminalizes (AC2 held). T-12622 ran 10:42:58Z, 192ee62 landed 10:44:07Z, and
    the audited 9c47e55 landed 11:49:30Z -> the run was already consumed -> NOT covered -> the pass is
    refused (AC1 held).

    FAIL-OPEN ON EVERY UNPROVABLE SHAPE — no `audited_commit`, no `tests_passed` row at all, no
    `commit_landed` row naming the audited commit, an unresolvable sha, an unparseable `ts`, or a
    run and a competing land recorded in the SAME SECOND (the journal records `ts` to the second, so
    their order is genuinely not recorded): return True (treated as covered). An engine that cannot PROVE the gap admits the pass EXACTLY as it does
    today, so this can never refuse a pass it has not earned the right to refuse.

    `resolve` is the injected sha resolver (the T-12385 discipline: a row may carry a SHORT sha and
    the subject a full one, so commit identity is RESOLVED, never string-compared). Pure; never
    raises."""
    if not audited_commit:
        return True                                     # fail-open: nothing to cover
    try:
        subject = (resolve(str(audited_commit)) or "").strip() if resolve else ""
    except Exception:                                   # noqa: BLE001 — a resolver fault fails OPEN
        return True
    if not subject:
        return True

    # JOURNAL ORDER IS THE TIE-BREAKER, and a REMAINING tie FAILS OPEN (audit-post pass-1 finding
    # fp1:cf77366310c965da, TRUE and fixed here). `ts` is recorded to the SECOND, so a run and a land
    # in the same second are ORDER-AMBIGUOUS at the recorded granularity: sorting on `ts` alone let a
    # land that in fact PRECEDED the run be selected as «the first land at-or-after it», returning
    # False — a refusal the engine cannot prove, which is exactly what the fail-open bound forbids.
    # So both halves below carry the row's journal INDEX, the sort is (ts, index) — stable, so equal
    # timestamps keep the order the journal actually recorded — and disjunct (B) fails OPEN when the
    # competing land shares the latest run's `ts` and the order is therefore not recorded at all.
    runs: list = []                                     # (ts, index) for tests_passed
    lands: list = []                                    # (ts, index, raw commit) for commit_landed
    for index, row in enumerate(rows or ()):
        if not isinstance(row, dict) or row.get("task_id") != task_id:
            continue
        kind = row.get("type")
        if kind not in ("tests_passed", "commit_landed"):
            continue
        ts = _parse_iso_ts(row.get("ts"))
        if ts is None:
            return True                                 # fail-open: an unparseable ledger ts
        if kind == "tests_passed":
            runs.append((ts, index))
            continue
        data = row.get("data") if isinstance(row.get("data"), dict) else {}
        commit = str(data.get("commit") or "").strip()
        if commit:
            lands.append((ts, index, commit))
    if not runs or not lands:
        return True                                     # fail-open: no ledger to read

    lands.sort(key=lambda land: (land[0], land[1]))
    audited = None
    for ts, index, commit in lands:
        try:
            resolved = (resolve(commit) or "").strip() if resolve else ""
        except Exception:                               # noqa: BLE001 — fail OPEN, never closed
            return True
        if resolved and resolved == subject:
            audited = (ts, index)                       # the FIRST land naming the subject
            break
    if audited is None:
        return True                                     # fail-open: the subject is not in the ledger

    # (A) CORRECTIVE COVERAGE — a run that POSTDATES the audited commit's own land.
    if any(run > audited[0] for run, _ in runs):
        return True

    # (B) ORDINARY COVERAGE — the audited commit is the FIRST land at-or-after the latest preceding run.
    preceding = [run for run in runs if (run[0], run[1]) <= audited]
    if not preceding:
        return True                                     # fail-open: no run precedes the subject
    latest = max(preceding, key=lambda run: (run[0], run[1]))
    first_after = next((land for land in lands if (land[0], land[1]) > (latest[0], latest[1])), None)
    if first_after is None:
        return True                                     # fail-open: unreachable by construction
    if first_after[0] == latest[0] and first_after[:2] != audited:
        # SAME-SECOND AMBIGUITY: the competing land and the run share a `ts`, so the journal does not
        # RECORD which came first. Unprovable -> fail OPEN, never a refusal on a guess.
        return True
    # IDENTITY, not timestamp equality: two lands can share a `ts` to the recorded second, and asking
    # «is the first land at-or-after the run the SUBJECT?» is the question — so re-resolve and compare
    # the sha, exactly as the subject side above did.
    try:
        first_resolved = (resolve(first_after[2]) or "").strip() if resolve else ""
    except Exception:                                   # noqa: BLE001 — fail OPEN, never closed
        return True
    if not first_resolved:
        return True                                     # fail-open: an unresolvable ledger sha
    return first_resolved == subject


def stage6_evidence_gap_facts(task_id, audited_commit, rows, resolve) -> dict:
    """T-12642 — the DISPLAY-ONLY facts behind a `stage6_evidence_covers_subject` refusal:
    `{"audited_commit", "latest_tests_passed_ts", "intervening_commit"}`.

    IT DECIDES NOTHING. The decision is `stage6_evidence_covers_subject`'s alone; this is the prose
    side, so a refusal can NAME the recorded run that does not cover the subject and the commit that
    consumed it instead of saying only which axis fired (the C1 audit-post B1 lesson: an aggregate
    cannot say which finding lacked which field). Kept SEPARATE from the predicate deliberately —
    the predicate's signature is the AC3 pin and stays a f(ledger) -> bool with nothing else in it.

    Every value is best-effort and may be None; the refusal message degrades to naming what it has.
    Pure f(rows); opens no journal; never raises."""
    facts = {"audited_commit": (str(audited_commit).strip() if audited_commit else None),
             "latest_tests_passed_ts": None, "intervening_commit": None}
    try:
        subject = (resolve(str(audited_commit)) or "").strip() if (resolve and audited_commit) else ""
        runs, lands = [], []
        for row in rows or ():
            if not isinstance(row, dict) or row.get("task_id") != task_id:
                continue
            ts = _parse_iso_ts(row.get("ts"))
            if ts is None:
                continue
            if row.get("type") == "tests_passed":
                runs.append((ts, row.get("ts")))
            elif row.get("type") == "commit_landed":
                data = row.get("data") if isinstance(row.get("data"), dict) else {}
                commit = str(data.get("commit") or "").strip()
                if commit:
                    lands.append((ts, commit))
        lands.sort(key=lambda pair: pair[0])
        audited_ts = next((ts for ts, c in lands
                           if subject and (resolve(c) or "").strip() == subject), None)
        preceding = [r for r in runs if audited_ts is None or r[0] <= audited_ts]
        if preceding:
            latest = max(preceding, key=lambda r: r[0])
            facts["latest_tests_passed_ts"] = latest[1]
            facts["intervening_commit"] = next(
                (c for ts, c in lands
                 if ts >= latest[0] and (audited_ts is None or ts < audited_ts)), None)
    except Exception:                                   # noqa: BLE001 — display never raises
        pass
    return facts


STAGE6_REMEDY_EVIDENCE_PLACEHOLDER = "<what ran, at which head>"


def stage6_remedy_command(task_id) -> str:
    """T-12763 (X-1506) — the ONE command the Stage-6 ledger-gap refusal tells an operator to run.

    THE SINGLE RENDERER of that command, so the AC1 tripwire round-trips the SAME string the refusal
    prints through the real parser rather than a hand-retyped copy of it.

    TWO defects it fixes, both in one previously hand-written line. (1) `task test` takes a
    POSITIONAL id — the `tt` parser (`bin/lib/cli_parser.py`) declares `tt.add_argument("task", ...)`
    and NO `--task` option — so the old `task test --run --task <tid>` failed argparse verbatim,
    before running anything. (2) `--evidence` is what RECORDS: a bare `--run` runs the suite and
    appends NO `tests_passed` row, deliberately (`bin/lib/task.py` — `--run` runs, `--evidence`
    records, SPEC-0025 / D-0033), so an operator who fixed only the flag spent the full subprocess
    suite and met a BYTE-IDENTICAL second refusal — the blind re-run CHARTER §P7 forbids.

    Pure f(str) -> str, no I/O, never raises."""
    return (f'bin/yitc-v2 task test {task_id} --run '
            f'--evidence "{STAGE6_REMEDY_EVIDENCE_PLACEHOLDER}"')


def stage6_evidence_rows_line(task_id, audited_commit, rows, resolve) -> str:
    """T-12763 — the DISPLAY line naming the `tests_passed` rows the ledger DOES hold for this task,
    so a SECOND refusal is distinguishable from the first: a reader sees whether their run recorded
    anything at all, instead of re-reading a byte-identical message.

    IT DECIDES NOTHING — the decision is `stage6_evidence_covers_subject`'s alone; this is the same
    display-only contract as `stage6_evidence_gap_facts` beside it. Pure f(rows); opens no journal;
    never raises.

    THE HEAD IS READ BACK OFF THE ROW'S OWN EVIDENCE TEXT. A `tests_passed` payload carries no
    structured head field (`{current_stage, evidence, acceptance}` + optional selection/duration
    keys) — the head lives in `data.evidence`, the runner's own attestation, which is EXACTLY what
    `stage6_remedy_command` above asks them to put there (`<what ran, at which head>`). So per row
    the rendering is `<ts> (evidence: "<the row's evidence text>")` — quoted verbatim, never
    paraphrased and never parsed for a sha, so it states what was attested and nothing more. The
    three alternatives were rejected on the record: recording a head sha ON the row is NEW STATE
    (this card's scope forbids it); DERIVING one from neighbouring `commit_landed` rows would print
    a GUESS as a fact, which a refusal an operator acts on may not do; and `head unrecorded` answers
    the criterion with an absence.

    FORWARD-COMPATIBLE: when a row itself records a head (`data.head` / `data.commit`) that sha is
    printed instead, so the day the payload carries one this line needs no edit.

    BOUNDED so one long attestation cannot swamp the refusal: at most the 5 most recent rows (newest
    last), prefixed `latest 5 of N` when truncated, each attestation clipped to ~80 chars with an
    ellipsis. `none found for this task` when the ledger holds no row for the task."""
    _MAX_ROWS = 5
    _MAX_EVIDENCE = 80
    try:
        found = []                                      # (ts_sort, ts_text, rendering)
        for row in (rows or ()):
            if not isinstance(row, dict) or row.get("task_id") != task_id:
                continue
            if row.get("type") != "tests_passed":
                continue
            ts_text = str(row.get("ts") or "").strip() or "ts unrecorded"
            data = row.get("data") if isinstance(row.get("data"), dict) else {}
            head = str(data.get("head") or data.get("commit") or "").strip()
            if head:
                detail = f"head {head}"
            else:
                evidence = str(data.get("evidence") or "").strip()
                if evidence:
                    if len(evidence) > _MAX_EVIDENCE:
                        evidence = evidence[:_MAX_EVIDENCE - 1].rstrip() + "…"
                    detail = f'evidence: "{evidence}"'
                else:
                    detail = "no evidence text recorded"
            found.append(f"{ts_text} ({detail})")
        if not found:
            return "none found for this task"
        # JOURNAL ORDER IS THE ORDER — `events.jsonl` is append-only, so the rows arrive oldest-first
        # and NEWEST LAST needs no sort. Not re-sorting on `ts` is deliberate: a row whose `ts` is
        # malformed keeps its recorded position instead of being dropped or floated, because this
        # line REPORTS the ledger, it never filters or re-judges it.
        total = len(found)
        shown = found[-_MAX_ROWS:]
        rendered = "; ".join(shown)
        if total > len(shown):
            return f"latest {len(shown)} of {total} — {rendered}"
        return rendered
    except Exception:                                   # noqa: BLE001 — display never raises
        return "none found for this task"


