# ── SPEC-0204 PLAN-GATE ARM — the ceiling ROW a plan gate never had (T-12335, card C-A/C-A2) ───────
# A plan gate emits `draft_checked` ONLY and has never emitted an `external_audit_completed` row, so
# the whole rule-1/2/3 reader chain — which keys off that row — had NO ceiling row to read for a plan
# target (measured read-only on <project> `otgruzki-design-parity-…` gate draft-specs and <project>
# `podklyuchenie-statistiki-…` gates specs-trial/trial; deviation
# `plan-gate-has-no-external-audit-completed-ceiling-row`, events.jsonl#ts=2026-09-10T13:34:29Z).
# These two functions supply it, and NOTHING ELSE: every helper below them is the TASK arm's,
# unchanged, called with `(slug, gate_id)` where it takes `(tid, stage)` — the pair is opaque to all
# of them.
#
# THE ROW'S `stage` IS THE GATE ID, never the audit TEMPLATE name. The gate id is the identity
# SPEC-0124 already established (`audit consult --gate` / `audit pre --plan --gate` take it) and the
# one `ceiling_ref` names; the template is the RECORD's name and is carried BESIDE it so the two
# identifiers stay told apart rather than colliding (the T-11250 discipline).

def plan_gate_ceiling_row_payload(slug, gate_id, template, audit, *, saved_to=None,
                                  repo_root=None) -> dict:
    """The `external_audit_completed` payload a plan-gate audit records BESIDE its `draft_checked`.

    The SAME event type + the SAME SPEC-0204 rule-1 ceiling-extension keys the task seam writes, so
    `_stage_audit_rows` / `_ceiling_row_of` / `row_residual_fingerprints` read a plan-gate row with no
    branch of their own: `target_kind: plan`, `target_id: <slug>` (which is what
    `_decide_row_is_for` matches on for a row carrying no envelope `task_id`), `stage: <gate_id>`,
    `passes` from the audit record's own counter, and `findings[]` each carrying an engine
    `finding_fingerprint` computed through `finding_key_of(slug, gate_id, f)`.

    `finding_key_of`, NOT `finding_fingerprint`, and that is the whole reason the reader and the row
    agree BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by two loops matching: today's plan-gate lens supplies none of
    the four structured fields rule 1's canonical tuple needs, so the key it yields is the marked
    READ-TIME DEGRADED `fp1d:` (whose tuple ADDS normalized `what` + `where`, which a plan-gate
    finding DOES carry, so distinct findings get distinct keys). `row_residual_fingerprints` recomputes
    the identical value off the same fields. The day the plan-gate lens supplies the structured fields
    this function starts stamping `fp1:` with no edit here.

    Pure f(args); never raises."""
    audit = audit if isinstance(audit, dict) else {}
    # T-12402 — a non-defect row (`severity: pass` …) is no finding of the ceiling row.
    findings = [f if isinstance(f, dict) else {} for f in (audit.get("findings") or ())
                if not is_non_defect_finding(f)]
    out = {
        "stage": str(gate_id),
        "verdict": audit.get("verdict"),
        "target_kind": "plan",
        "target_id": str(slug),
        "gate": str(gate_id),
        "stage_template": str(template),
        "findings_count": len(findings),
        "findings": [{**f, "finding_fingerprint": finding_key_of(slug, gate_id, f,
                                                                repo_root=repo_root)}
                     for f in findings],
    }
    passes = audit.get("passes")
    if isinstance(passes, int):
        out["passes"] = passes
    if saved_to:
        out["saved_to"] = str(saved_to)
    return out


plan_gate_synthetic_ceiling_row = audit_ceiling.plan_gate_synthetic_ceiling_row  # T-12706 host residue — the body now lives in `bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py#plan_gate_synthetic_ceiling_row` (inject-free: re-export alias)


def plan_gate_response_malformed(findings, parse_notes) -> list:
    """SPEC-0204 rule 1's parse floor, PLAN-GATE arm (T-12335) — is this gate response one whose
    findings were recovered by the STAGE-3 RAW SINGLE-LINE BULLET fallback?

    Returns `[{"index": int, "finding": dict}]` — the recovered rows — and the falsy `[]` otherwise.

    WHAT IT JUDGES, and why it is NOT rule 1's task-side floor. The task floor
    (`red_findings_missing_contract_fields`) refuses a RED finding lacking
    `criterion_ref|class_id` / `locator` / `failing_input`. Imposing THAT on a plan gate would refuse
    every RED the current plan-gate lens can produce — its findings carry `{severity, what, where,
    fix}` by contract — and would wedge the two consumer plans this arm exists to unwedge. So the
    plan-gate floor is the MALFORMED-RESPONSE floor instead: the X-1330 envelope in which strict YAML
    and the structured multi-line parser BOTH failed and `parse_audit_verdict` fell back to scraping
    single-line bullets into `{severity, what: "<bullet text>"}` dicts it calls, in its own words,
    «likely misleading». That response is not a disagreement about the plan; it is a transport-shaped
    parse failure, and on 2026-09-09 it consumed the same bounded budget as a real one (4 invocations,
    ~10 min).

    KEYED ON THE ONE DECLARED MARKER `RAW_BULLET_FALLBACK_MARKER`, which is also what the fallback's
    own message is built from — so the producer and this floor cannot silently name different shapes.
    Stage-2 STRUCTURED recovery is NOT malformed and is recorded unchanged: it recognises a compliant
    finding through a closed allowlist and yields the same dict a clean stage-1 parse would.

    VERDICT-AGNOSTIC, unlike the task floor. A stage-3 scrape says nothing trustworthy about WHICH
    verdict the auditor reached either, and the response is refused whatever word was parsed.

    PURE: it judges shape, refuses nothing, and writes nothing — the REFUSAL belongs to the use site
    (`lessons/fail-closed-belongs-to-the-reader-not-the-parser.md`), which is where the «no pass
    spent, no record written» half lives."""
    if RAW_BULLET_FALLBACK_MARKER not in str(parse_notes or ""):
        return []
    rows = [f if isinstance(f, dict) else {} for f in (findings or ())]
    if not rows:
        return []
    return [{"index": i, "finding": f} for i, f in enumerate(rows)]


#: T-12735 — the `read_gate_refused.kind` every unactionable-response refusal carries, at all three
#: doors. ONE spelling: the T-12335 plan-gate arm already emitted it, and `test_t12382` pins it.
UNACTIONABLE_RESPONSE_REFUSAL_KIND = "response-malformed"
#: The two per-row REASONS — the stage-3 scrape (T-12335's arm, reason text unchanged) and the
#: empty-narrative arm this card adds. A refusal row names its reason so a later reader can tell a
#: transport-shaped parse failure from an auditor that returned a bare severity list.
UNACTIONABLE_REASON_RAW_BULLET = "raw_bullet_fallback_findings"
UNACTIONABLE_REASON_CONTENTLESS = "contentless_findings"


def contentless_findings(findings) -> list:
    """T-12735 (<project> X-1494) — the EMPTY-NARRATIVE arm of the unactionable-response floor: which
    findings carry NO non-blank value in any `CONTENT_FINDING_FIELDS` key?

    Returns `[{"index": int, "finding": dict}]` — the offending rows — and the falsy `[]` when every
    finding says something. A non-dict finding is contentless by construction (it has no fields).

    WHAT IT JUDGES. Not the response contract (rule 1's structured-fields floor, TASK-RED-only) and
    not the parser stage (the raw-bullet arm beside it): only whether the finding, AS IT WOULD BE
    RECORDED, gives a later reader anything to act on. The saved YAML is what every downstream
    consumer reads — the absorption trail, the re-check, the SPEC-0204 rule 1 residual set — and a
    finding with empty bodies there is an UNDECIDABLE residual: the Controller would be asked to
    record a typed decision about nothing. Measured 2026-09-18 on <project>: RED with four such
    findings, the real bodies alive only inside `notes:`.

    VERDICT-AGNOSTIC and PURE, like `plan_gate_response_malformed`: it judges shape, refuses nothing
    and writes nothing — the REFUSAL belongs to the use site
    (`lessons/fail-closed-belongs-to-the-reader-not-the-parser.md`). The key names are read FROM the
    one declaration `CONTENT_FINDING_FIELDS`, never re-listed here.

    THE ROW CARRIES THE FINDING AS GIVEN, never a normalized stand-in (audit-post RED, this card). A
    non-dict finding is inspected THROUGH an empty mapping — it has no fields to read — but the row
    returns the ORIGINAL value, because the whole point of the row is that the discarded content
    survives in a recorded artifact (the T-12646 lesson). Substituting `{}` there would destroy
    exactly the text the refusal promises to preserve."""
    out = []
    for i, f in enumerate(findings or ()):
        fields = f if isinstance(f, dict) else {}
        if not any(str(fields.get(k) or "").strip() for k in CONTENT_FINDING_FIELDS):
            out.append({"index": i, "finding": f})
    return out


def unactionable_findings(findings, parse_notes) -> list:
    """T-12735 — THE ONE unactionable-response judgement the three record doors read (`plan stage`
    gates, `plan check`, `cmd_audit`'s genuine-response branch): the union of the stage-3 raw-bullet
    arm (`plan_gate_response_malformed`, T-12335) and the empty-narrative arm (`contentless_findings`).

    Returns `[{"index": int, "finding": dict, "reason": str}]` ordered by index, ONE row per offending
    finding — a finding flagged by both arms carries the raw-bullet reason (the more specific cause:
    a scrape is why it is contentless). Falsy `[]` when nothing is unactionable.

    HOMED ONCE (the T-12725 rule — two inline copies of one class judgement had silently diverged in
    this file before): every door calls THIS, resolved through the module attribute at call time,
    never its own copy of either arm. PURE — refusal and journaling stay at the use site."""
    by_index: dict = {}
    for row in plan_gate_response_malformed(findings, parse_notes):
        by_index[row["index"]] = {**row, "reason": UNACTIONABLE_REASON_RAW_BULLET}
    for row in contentless_findings(findings):
        by_index.setdefault(row["index"], {**row, "reason": UNACTIONABLE_REASON_CONTENTLESS})
    return [by_index[i] for i in sorted(by_index)]


#: T-13158 — the bound on the auditor output a record / refusal row keeps (plan check / plan gate
#: notes, `response-malformed` rows). Large enough that a 27-finding response survives whole (the
#: chartdesk 57ec009 case lost 25 of 27 to a 2000-char tail); the tail is kept, a cut is marked.
AUDITOR_OUTPUT_MAX_CHARS = 60000
UNPARSED_FINDINGS_REASON = "findings_block_unparsed"
_FINDINGS_KEY_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"^(\s*)findings:\s*$")
_LIST_ITEM_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"^(\s*)-\s")


def bounded_auditor_output(text) -> str:
    """T-13158 — `text` bounded to AUDITOR_OUTPUT_MAX_CHARS (tail kept, truncation marked). Pure."""
    return audit_consult._bounded_raw_source(str(text or ""),
                                             RAW_FINDINGS_SOURCE_MAX_CHARS=AUDITOR_OUTPUT_MAX_CHARS)


def _findings_block_item_count(stdout: str):
    """T-13158 — top-level item count of the LAST `findings:` block in `stdout` (items = `- ` lines
    at the block's first-item indent), or None when no block with items exists. Pure."""
    lines = str(stdout or "").splitlines()
    starts = [i for i, ln in enumerate(lines) if _FINDINGS_KEY_LINE_RE.match(ln)]
    if not starts:
        return None
    key_indent = len(_FINDINGS_KEY_LINE_RE.match(lines[starts[-1]]).group(1))
    item_indent, count = None, 0
    for ln in lines[starts[-1] + 1:]:
        if not ln.strip() or ln.lstrip().startswith("#"):
            continue
        indent = len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())
        m = _LIST_ITEM_LINE_RE.match(ln)
        if item_indent is None:
            if not m or indent < key_indent:
                break
            item_indent = indent
        if m and indent == item_indent:
            count += 1
        elif indent < item_indent or (indent <= key_indent and not m):
            break
    return count or None


def findings_block_parse_failure(verdict, findings, parse_notes, stdout):
    """T-13158 — non-None (the failure text) only for a YELLOW/RED whose findings did NOT come from a
    clean yaml-strict parse AND whose last `findings:` block lists MORE items than were recovered:
    zero recovery (the chartdesk 0-of-27 YELLOW) and partial stage-2 recovery are refused; a complete
    stage-2 recovery (T-12735 <project>, 4 of 4) records unchanged (SPEC-0204 rule 9(b)). Pure."""
    if str(verdict or "").upper() not in ("YELLOW", "RED"):
        return None
    if "findings parsed via yaml-strict" in str(parse_notes or ""):
        return None
    listed = _findings_block_item_count(stdout)
    recovered = len(findings or ())
    if listed is None or listed <= recovered:
        return None
    err = "no findings: block parsed"
    try:
        import yaml
        text = str(stdout or "")
        m = list(re.finditer(r"(?m)^\s*findings:\s*$", text))
        yaml.safe_load(audit_consult._extract_fenced_block(text[m[-1].start():]) if m else text)
    except Exception as e:   # noqa: BLE001 — the failure TEXT is what we report
        err = " ".join(str(e).split())[:400]
    return f"{err} ({recovered} of {listed} findings recovered)"


def unparsed_findings_refusal(verdict, failure, *, stdout, verb: str) -> tuple:
    """T-13158 — the refusal shape for a non-GREEN verdict whose findings block did not parse:
    `(payload_fields, message)`; the payload carries the bounded auditor output so the findings stay
    recoverable. No record, no pass spent (SPEC-0204 rule 9(b) arm iii). PURE."""
    payload = {
        "kind": UNACTIONABLE_RESPONSE_REFUSAL_KIND,
        "reason": UNPARSED_FINDINGS_REASON,
        "verdict_parsed": verdict,
        "parse_error": failure,
        "auditor_output": bounded_auditor_output(stdout),
    }
    message = (
        f"{verb}: the auditor returned {verdict} but its findings block did NOT parse — {failure}. "
        f"`{UNPARSED_FINDINGS_REASON}`: recording it would store a {verdict} with findings lost. "
        f"REFUSED AT PARSE: no record written, no ceiling row emitted and the pass counter unchanged "
        f"(SPEC-0204 rule 9(b); T-13158). Re-run. The auditor's raw text is preserved (bounded) in "
        f"this refusal's journal row (`auditor_output`).")
    return payload, message


def unactionable_findings_refusal(rows, *, findings_count: int, verb: str, stdout=None) -> tuple:
    """T-12735 — the ONE refusal SHAPE for an unactionable response, so the three doors cannot word
    or journal it differently: returns `(payload_fields, message)`.

    `payload_fields` are the `read_gate_refused` data keys common to every door — `kind`, `reason`
    (the FIRST offender's reason when all agree, else `mixed`), `findings_refused` (per row: 1-based
    `index` matching the printed text, `reason`, and the finding's mapping VERBATIM — the discarded
    text must live in a RECORDED artifact, the T-12646 lesson), `findings_offending`, and
    `findings_count`. The door adds its own target / stage / pass fields and appends.

    `message` names each offending finding by 1-based index and reason and states the semantics
    every door shares: no record written, no ceiling row, the pass counter unchanged — a response
    that cannot be acted on is not a verdict about the subject and must not spend the bounded
    budget (SPEC-0204 rule 9(b), widened to all three doors by T-12735). PURE."""
    rows = list(rows or ())
    reasons = {r.get("reason") for r in rows}
    reason = next(iter(reasons)) if len(reasons) == 1 else "mixed"
    payload = {
        "kind": UNACTIONABLE_RESPONSE_REFUSAL_KIND,
        "reason": reason,
        "findings_refused": [
            {"index": r["index"] + 1, "reason": r.get("reason"), "finding": r.get("finding")}
            for r in rows],
        "findings_offending": len(rows),
        "findings_count": int(findings_count or 0),
    }
    if stdout is not None:   # T-13158 — makes the «raw text preserved» sentence below true
        payload["auditor_output"] = bounded_auditor_output(stdout)
    def _head(finding) -> str:
        """The 80-char reading convenience for the message line — NEVER the record of the content
        (that is `findings_refused[].finding`, verbatim). A non-dict finding has no `what`, so its
        own text is the head."""
        if isinstance(finding, dict):
            return str(finding.get("what") or "?")[:80]
        return str(finding if finding is not None else "?")[:80]

    named = "; ".join(
        f"finding #{r['index'] + 1} ({_head(r.get('finding'))}) — {r.get('reason')}"
        for r in rows)
    message = (
        f"{verb}: the auditor response is UNACTIONABLE — {len(rows)} of {int(findings_count or 0)} "
        f"finding(s) carry nothing a later reader could act on ({named}). "
        f"`{UNACTIONABLE_REASON_RAW_BULLET}` = strict YAML AND the structured multi-line parser both "
        f"failed and the findings are the raw single-line bullet scrape `parse_audit_verdict` itself "
        f"calls «likely misleading»; `{UNACTIONABLE_REASON_CONTENTLESS}` = no non-blank value in any of "
        f"{', '.join(CONTENT_FINDING_FIELDS)}. REFUSED AT PARSE: no record written, no ceiling row "
        f"emitted and the pass counter unchanged — a response nobody can act on is not a verdict "
        f"about the subject and must not spend the bounded budget (SPEC-0204 rule 9(b); T-12735, "
        f"a consumer's X-1494). Re-run; the auditor is asked for the same judgement with its bodies in a "
        f"parseable envelope. The auditor's raw text is preserved in this refusal's journal row.")
    return payload, message


# ── SPEC-0204 rule 2 — `audit decide`: the ceiling-decision WRITER (T-12287, card C2) ──────────────
# C1 (T-12293) declared the payload, the fingerprint and the row-level residual read path; NOTHING
# wrote a `ceiling_decision` row. This block is that writer, and NOTHING ELSE: it appends ONE
# append-only journal row per residual, Controller-only, with the authorizing owner directive RESOLVED
# at write. No store, no card prose, no marker, no hook (owner directives
# events.jsonl#ts=2026-09-08T14:12:55Z / 13:07:17Z).
#
# WHY IT IS NOT A STAGE-WORK VERB (so it carries no stage read-check / stage-correspondence guard,
# `patterns/verb-design.md §Stage-work-verb BUILD recipe`): it is a Controller record ABOUT a stage
# that has already spent its ceiling, callable from the MAIN checkout with no worktree at all
# (D-0049 — the row is folded into main by the next `land`, SPEC-0168). Gating it on a lifecycle
# stage would make the Controller open a worktree to record a decision, which is the opposite of what
# rule 2 specifies. Its refusals are the AUTHORITY axes below, each its own `read_gate_refused` kind.

#: The rule-2 per-finding dispositions. A card-TERMINAL disposition is never a `ceiling_decision`
#: (rule 3 routes those to `task update --status parked|wont-do` / `worktree park` / a NEW card).
CEILING_DECISION_DISPOSITIONS = audit_ceiling.CEILING_DECISION_DISPOSITIONS  # T-12706: moved module-local to bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py (re-export alias)

#: The TELEMETRY SENTINEL `_plan_card_set_fingerprint` (bin/lib/plan.py) degrades to when it cannot
#: READ the cut — its contract is "never crash the gate verb", so a read failure returns this stable
#: hash rather than raising. It is therefore a CONSTANT a caller could type, which is why the rule-9(d)
#: `fix` route treats it as NO signature and refuses (T-12615, audit-pre fp1:dd744a3bf8b91729). DERIVED
#: from the helper's own expression, never re-spelled as a literal digest: if that expression ever
#: changes, this constant follows it rather than silently ceasing to match.
PLAN_CARD_SET_FINGERPRINT_ERROR = audit_ceiling.PLAN_CARD_SET_FINGERPRINT_ERROR  # T-12706: moved module-local to bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py (re-export alias)

#: The rule-2 directive LOCATOR form — `events.jsonl#ts=<ISO>` (D-0030's materialized-journal
#: citation, the only form the handbook mints). ONE pattern, TWO reading modes, and the difference
#: is load-bearing: the `--directive` ARGUMENT is read with `.fullmatch()` (the whole value must BE a
#: locator), while a `controller-delegated` row's own PROSE is scanned with `.finditer()` (a locator
#: cited mid-sentence). The timestamp is pinned to the journal's exact instant format rather than a
#: loose character class — under a loose one, `events.jsonl#ts=<ISO>-trailing` matched the ISO PREFIX
#: and the trailing text was silently discarded, so a malformed locator RESOLVED (audit-post finding
#: 5). Anchoring the argument is what makes trailing or surrounding text a `directive-unresolved`.
_DIRECTIVE_LOCATOR_RE = audit_ceiling._DIRECTIVE_LOCATOR_RE  # T-12706: moved module-local to bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py (re-export alias)

#: The quoted spans an `accept` reason may quote the authorizing words with. Rule 2: «an `accept`
#: reason MUST quote the authorizing words» — a «…» or "…" span present VERBATIM in the resolved
#: directive's text. Typographic double quotes are admitted alongside ASCII ones for the same reason
#: `_FP_QUOTE_MAP` exists: the owner's prose may round-trip through a renderer that curls them.
_ACCEPT_QUOTE_RE = audit_ceiling._ACCEPT_QUOTE_RE  # T-12706: moved module-local to bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py (re-export alias)

#: T-12617 (E-0054, X-1427) — the `audit decide --from-stdin` x argv content-flag conflict table, in
#: the (attr, cli_flag, stdin_key, absent_value) shape `textutil.stdin_argv_flag_conflicts` reads.
#:
#: WHY THIS VERB JOINED THE FAMILY LAST AND MOST URGENTLY: `--reason` is PROSE, and the row it lands
#: in is an append-only `ceiling_decision` (rule 4 — one decision per (ceiling_ref, fingerprint),
#: NEVER edited). Measured on <project> 2026-09-15: a `--reason` quoting a command in backticks was
#: command-substituted by the shell BEFORE the verb ran; the script EXECUTED against a live sandbox
#: and its stdout was written into the row. The result is a 7947-character governance decision whose
#: opening sentence breaks mid-clause into machine output, and which CANNOT be corrected. Same class
#: as `blocked-on-land` (T-11008), and the table is deliberately its shape.
#:
#: FOUR ROWS, and the omissions are the design. Only the CONTENT-bearing flags are here — the prose
#: `--reason` and the three short values that accompany a disposition. The TARGET and ROUTING flags
#: (`--task` / `--plan` / `--stage` / `--gate` / `--finding` / `--disposition`) are argv-ONLY by
#: intent: they are id-shaped, carry no prose, ride no shell hazard, and every refusal message in
#: this verb quotes them back to the caller. Leaving them out of `recognised` is what makes
#: `classify_stdin_keys` REFUSE one sent via stdin, by name, instead of dropping it in silence
#: (T-11742 / X-1157) — the fail-closed direction.
AUDIT_DECIDE_STDIN_CONFLICTING_ARGV_FLAGS = (
    ("reason", "--reason", "reason", None),
    ("directive", "--directive", "directive", None),
    ("receiving", "--receiving", "receiving", None),
    ("evidence", "--evidence", "evidence", None),
)

#: The stdin keys the fold-back below reads — the same set, by DERIVATION rather than a second
#: hand-list (CHARTER §P5): `recognised` and the conflict table must never disagree.
AUDIT_DECIDE_STDIN_KEYS = tuple(k for _a, _f, k, _v in AUDIT_DECIDE_STDIN_CONFLICTING_ARGV_FLAGS)

#: `captured_via` value of a directive row the CONTROLLER wrote under an owner delegation. Rule 2:
#: such a row «resolves ONLY through its chain» — it must cite the owner row that delegated it.
_CONTROLLER_DELEGATED = audit_ceiling._CONTROLLER_DELEGATED  # T-12706: moved module-local to bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py (re-export alias)


def _decide_row_is_for(row, tid) -> bool:
    """Is this journal row about task `tid`? The task id sits on the ENVELOPE (`task_id`) for most
    emits and in `data.target_id` for the audit family; a row is this task's if EITHER says so.
    Pure; tolerant of a non-dict row."""
    if not isinstance(row, dict):
        return False
    data = row.get("data") if isinstance(row.get("data"), dict) else {}
    # `plan_slug` is the THIRD carrier, added by the SPEC-0204 plan-gate arm (T-12335): a
    # `ceiling_decision` on a plan gate carries `task_id: None` on BOTH the envelope and the payload
    # (there is no task), and names its target in `plan_slug`. Without this arm the duplicate check of
    # `audit decide --plan` would see no prior decision for ANY plan and rule 4's «one decision per
    # (ceiling_ref, fingerprint), never edited» would be unenforceable on the plan axis. Harmless for a
    # task row, which never carries the key.
    return (row.get("task_id") == tid or data.get("target_id") == tid
            or data.get("plan_slug") == tid)


def _stage_audit_rows(rows, tid, stage) -> list:
    """The `external_audit_completed` rows for THIS (task, stage), in journal order.

    `data.stage` is EXACTLY `pre` / `post` for a task audit; the consult family writes
    `consult-pre` / `consult-post` on the same event type, so the exact match is what keeps a consult
    round out of the ceiling-row resolution. Pure f(rows) — the caller supplies the rows from the
    SEGMENT-AWARE reader (`_iter_events` → `journal.segment_rows`, SPEC-0190 rule 4); this function
    never opens the journal itself, so it can never become a second, segment-blind read path."""
    out = []
    for row in rows or ():
        if not isinstance(row, dict) or row.get("type") != "external_audit_completed":
            continue
        data = row.get("data") if isinstance(row.get("data"), dict) else {}
        if data.get("stage") != stage or not _decide_row_is_for(row, tid):
            continue
        out.append(row)
    return out


def _ceiling_decision_rows(rows, tid) -> list:
    """The `ceiling_decision` rows already recorded for this task, in journal order. The duplicate
    check reads these — append-only means a decision is never edited (rule 4). Pure f(rows)."""
    return [r for r in rows or ()
            if isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("type") == "ceiling_decision" and _decide_row_is_for(r, tid)]


def suspected_echo_findings(findings, stage_rows, decisions, *, tid, stage) -> list:
    """SPEC-0204 rule 3 (ADDITIVE, T-12899) — the findings of a RED that LOOK LIKE a decided residual
    re-raised under a new key. REPORT-ONLY: the result names a suspicion, it never binds, overrules or
    drops a finding, and the caller changes no verdict, `passes`, row or finding on it.

    WHY IT EXISTS (<project> T-0752 / X-1549): a decision binds one engine fingerprint, and the
    fingerprint hashes `locator` + `failing_input`. An auditor that re-describes an adjudicated defect
    therefore raises it as a NEW finding and a fresh RED, and nothing on the record said so. Rule 3
    forbids MATCHING on a coarser key (it merged distinct findings on real output), so this only
    points the Controller at the prior decision; it decides with an ordinary `audit decide`.

    THE KEY is rule 1's own criterion reduction — the upper-cased `AC<n>` token of `criterion_ref`
    (`_FP_AC_TOKEN_RE`, the criterion half of `_fp_key`). A class_id-only finding carries none: a
    class spans many unrelated defects, so a class match would be noise.

    T-13431 (<project> X-1549, owner option A) — TWO MORE REPORT ARMS beside the AC token, still never a
    match: `names-receiving-card` (the finding's own text names a decided residual's receiving card
    or its fingerprint as a whole token — the 11:59Z T-0752 auditor wrote «deferred to T-0748» and set
    no `echo_of`) and `same-criterion-locator` (its criterion — the normalized `criterion_ref` as
    written, or rule 1's reduction of it, AC token else class_id — AND its normalized `locator` equal a
    decided residual's recorded ones, which reaches a non-AC criterion the AC-token arm never sees). Each prior names
    the arm(s) that raised it under `match`. Binding stays exact-key or verified `echo_of` only.

    Returns `[{finding_fingerprint, criterion_ref, locator, prior: [{finding_fingerprint,
    disposition, ceiling_ref, receiving_task, match}]}]`, in finding order. A finding whose own fingerprint
    is decided, or whose `echo_of` names a decided fingerprint, is already bound by rule 3 and is
    never a suspicion. Decisions are this task's rows at THIS stage only; a decision's criterion is
    resolved through the `findings[]` / `late_findings[]` of the (task, stage) rows, since a
    `ceiling_decision` payload carries no criterion. Pure f(rows); never raises."""
    def _ac(cref):
        m = _FP_AC_TOKEN_RE.search(cref) if isinstance(cref, str) else None
        return f"AC{m.group(1)}" if m else None

    def _norm(v):
        return " ".join(v.split()).lower() if isinstance(v, str) and v.strip() else None

    def _crits(e):
        # the criterion as written (normalized) AND rule 1's own reduction (AC token, else class_id)
        return {c for c in (_norm(e.get("criterion_ref")), _ac(e.get("criterion_ref")),
                            _norm(e.get("class_id"))) if c}

    def _text(f):
        return " ".join(str(v) for k, v in f.items()
                        if k not in ("finding_fingerprint", "echo_of") and isinstance(v, str))

    try:
        decided = {}
        for r in decisions or ():
            if not isinstance(r, dict) or r.get("type") != "ceiling_decision" \
                    or not _decide_row_is_for(r, tid):
                continue
            d = r.get("data") if isinstance(r.get("data"), dict) else {}
            fp = d.get("finding_fingerprint")
            if d.get("stage") == stage and d.get("task_id") == tid and isinstance(fp, str) and fp \
                    and fp not in decided:
                decided[fp] = d
        if not decided:
            return []
        by_key: dict = {}
        by_pair: dict = {}
        for row in stage_rows or ():
            data = row.get("data") if isinstance(row, dict) and isinstance(row.get("data"), dict) else {}
            for e in list(data.get("findings") or ()) + list(data.get("late_findings") or ()):
                if not isinstance(e, dict):
                    continue
                fp, key = e.get("finding_fingerprint"), _ac(e.get("criterion_ref"))
                if key and fp in decided and fp not in by_key.setdefault(key, []):
                    by_key[key].append(fp)
                loc = _norm(e.get("locator"))
                for c in (_crits(e) if fp in decided and loc else ()):
                    if fp not in by_pair.setdefault((c, loc), []):
                        by_pair[(c, loc)].append(fp)
        out = []
        for f in findings or ():
            if not isinstance(f, dict):
                continue
            if f.get("finding_fingerprint") in decided or str(f.get("echo_of") or "").strip() in decided:
                continue
            match: dict = {}
            for p in by_key.get(_ac(f.get("criterion_ref")) or "", ()):
                match.setdefault(p, []).append("criterion-key")
            loc = _norm(f.get("locator"))
            for c in (_crits(f) if loc else ()):
                for p in by_pair.get((c, loc), ()):
                    if "same-criterion-locator" not in match.get(p, ()):
                        match.setdefault(p, []).append("same-criterion-locator")
            text = _text(f)
            for p, d in decided.items():
                recv = str(d.get("receiving_task") or "").strip()
                if (recv and _prose_names_token(text, recv)) or _prose_names_token(text, p):
                    match.setdefault(p, []).append("names-receiving-card")
            if not match:
                continue
            out.append({"finding_fingerprint": f.get("finding_fingerprint"),
                        "criterion_ref": f.get("criterion_ref"), "locator": f.get("locator"),
                        "prior": [{"finding_fingerprint": p,
                                   "disposition": decided[p].get("disposition"),
                                   "ceiling_ref": decided[p].get("ceiling_ref"),
                                   "receiving_task": decided[p].get("receiving_task"),
                                   "match": arms}
                                  for p, arms in match.items()]})
        return out
    except Exception:   # noqa: BLE001 — a report must never break the audit it annotates
        return []


def counterless_green_row_passes(row, record):
    """T-13561 — the SPEC-0124 counter of a GREEN completion row that STATES NONE, read from ITS OWN
    saved record's explicit `passes` — or None.

    Until T-13561 the write site stamped `passes` only on a row that also carried `findings[]`, so a
    real GREEN pass that followed a RED one wrote a counterless row and `_ceiling_row_of` ranked it
    below the RED it superseded (measured on T-13461, 2026-10-05: RED `passes: 1`, then GREEN with no
    counter beside a record stating GREEN `passes: 2`). Those rows stay in the append-only journal, so
    the reader resolves them rather than anything rewriting them.

    THE RECORD MUST BE THIS ROW'S RECORD — the T-12319 doctrine `row_residual_fingerprints` holds,
    applied to the counter: both sides GREEN, the record not a currency pass's (it is no pass of the
    card, T-12422), and the subject agreeing wherever BOTH sides state one (`plan_fingerprint` at
    pre, verbatim; at post the record's `commit` starting with the row's short one). Silence on
    either side is not a contradiction. GREEN ONLY, and that bound is load-bearing: a rule-5 no-answer
    ABORT row is counterless BY DESIGN and must keep ranking below every counted row. A row that
    states its own counter is never re-read. Pure; never raises."""
    data = (row.get("data") if isinstance(row, dict) and isinstance(row.get("data"), dict) else {})
    if isinstance(data.get("passes"), int) or not isinstance(record, dict):
        return None
    stage = str(data.get("stage") or "").strip().lower()
    if stage not in ("pre", "post"):
        return None
    if (str(data.get("verdict") or "").strip().upper() != "GREEN"
            or str(record.get("verdict") or "").strip().upper() != "GREEN"):
        return None
    if record.get("reaudit_after_close") is True or str(record.get("basis") or "") == CURRENCY_BASIS:
        return None
    key = "plan_fingerprint" if stage == "pre" else "commit"
    row_subject, rec_subject = str(data.get(key) or "").strip(), str(record.get(key) or "").strip()
    if row_subject and rec_subject and not (row_subject == rec_subject if stage == "pre"
                                            else rec_subject.startswith(row_subject)):
        return None
    return explicit_record_passes(record)


def _ceiling_row_of(stage_rows, record=None):
    """THE ceiling row of a (task, stage) — the row a decision binds to — or None.

    Selected by the HIGHEST recorded `passes`, never by physical journal position. The journal is
    append-only + union-merged and SPEC-0190 rule 5 promises multiset identity plus partition-stable
    order, explicitly NOT positional identity: rows arrive out of `ts` order, so a legal
    re-segmentation can move which row is physically last. Reading `stage_rows[-1]` would therefore
    let the ceiling a decision binds to depend on how the journal happens to be partitioned — a
    silent, un-witnessable difference in `ceiling_ref`.

    `passes` is the right key on its own merits, not merely as an order-free substitute: SPEC-0204
    rule 2 states the SPEC-0124 counter «is unique and monotonic per (task, stage)», which is exactly
    the property `ceiling_ref`'s `pass-<N>` already relies on. A row that records NO counter ranks
    below every row that does (a legacy row can never displace a contract-era ceiling row), and the
    remaining ties fall to `ts` and then to the row's own canonical bytes — a TOTAL order, so the
    answer is a function of the row SET alone and never of the order they were read in.

    A `basis: CURRENCY_BASIS` ROW IS NEVER THE CEILING ROW (T-12370). An audit-currency re-audit is a
    CHECK of the merged tree, not one of the card's counted passes — it consumes no pass and its
    findings are not the card's residual set — so a decision must never bind to it. This is a BELT
    over construction rather than the only guard: the write site records no `passes` on a currency
    row, which already ranks it -1 below every counter-bearing row (the same property the rule-5
    no-answer row relies on). The belt is what holds for a LEGACY or hand-written row that carries
    both, which is precisely the shape a fail-closed reader must not let win.

    WHEN EVERY ROW IS A CURRENCY ROW THE ANSWER IS None, and that is the honest one rather than a
    fallback: the stage has recorded no counted pass, so there is no ceiling row for a decision to
    bind to, and every caller already treats None as exactly that (`no_completion_row` ->
    `below-ceiling`). Falling back to the unfiltered maximum here would re-admit the very row this
    filter exists to exclude, and would do it silently.

    `record` (T-13561) — the saved audit record of this (task, stage), INJECTED by the task-arm
    seams that hold one. It answers ONLY for a GREEN row that states no counter, and only when it is
    that row's own record (`counterless_green_row_passes`); omitted, every row ranks as before."""
    if not stage_rows:
        return None

    def _rank(row):
        data = row.get("data") if isinstance(row.get("data"), dict) else {}
        passes = data.get("passes")
        if not isinstance(passes, int):
            passes = counterless_green_row_passes(row, record)
        return (int(passes) if isinstance(passes, int) else -1,
                str(row.get("ts") or ""),
                json.dumps(row, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False, default=str))

    counted = [r for r in stage_rows
               if str(((r.get("data") if isinstance(r.get("data"), dict) else {})
                       .get("basis")) or "") != CURRENCY_BASIS]
    if not counted:
        return None
    return max(counted, key=_rank)


def terminal_ceiling_row(target_kind, stage_rows, rows, target_id, target_key):
    """THE SPEC-0204 rule-4 TERMINAL row of a (target, key) — or None when the stage is still open.

    ONE reading, consulted by BOTH the reader and the writer of a ceiling decision (T-12613, X-1433 /
    X-1434): `cmd_audit`'s at-ceiling arm refuses a further pass `ceiling-terminal` off this row, and
    `cmd_audit_decide` refuses to RECORD a decision against it — a decision whose only consumer is a
    pass this row has already ended can never be spent. Before T-12613 the writer did not read this
    state at all, so the decision recorded cleanly and read to the operator as a route.

    A `plan` TARGET ANSWERS None BY CONTRACT, never by omission (SPEC-0204 rule 9(f), T-12505): a
    plan-gate budget is DECISION-BOUNDED — a rule-3 RED at a gate is that gate's NEW ceiling row and
    the next `plan stage <NEXT> --on-decisions` round is admitted on fresh decisions over it
    (`bin/lib/plan.py#_run_plan_gate_audit`: «the task arm's rule-4 terminal is NOT applied here»).
    That is exactly the reading `cmd_audit` already performs — its whole terminal arm is gated
    `target_kind == "task"` — lifted INTO the predicate so a caller never spells the guard itself.

    For a `task` target: PHANTOM rule-3 rows are dropped first through `_on_decisions_row_is_real`
    (T-12310 — a `basis: on-decisions` row no `audit_prompt_sent` vouches for is a pass that never
    ran and must not read as a terminal verdict; the drop is idempotent, so a caller that has
    already partitioned them out gets the same answer), then the first surviving row whose `basis`
    is the rule-3 pass or the ONE bounded absorption re-audit (T-12376) and whose `verdict` is RED is
    the terminal row. Pure f(rows); never raises; opens no journal of its own (SPEC-0190 rule 10 —
    the caller passes the rows the fold already folded)."""
    if str(target_kind or "") != "task":
        return None
    real = [r for r in stage_rows or ()
            if isinstance(r, dict)
            and _on_decisions_row_is_real(r, stage_rows, rows, target_id, target_key)]
    return next(
        (r for r in real
         if str(((r.get("data") if isinstance(r.get("data"), dict) else {}).get("basis")) or "")
         # T-13260 — a RED on the ONE post-GREEN merge-integration pass ends audit-post the same way.
         in (ON_DECISIONS_BASIS, ON_DECISIONS_ABSORPTION_BASIS, audit_ceiling.MERGE_INTEGRATION_BASIS)
         and str(((r.get("data") if isinstance(r.get("data"), dict) else {}).get("verdict")) or "")
         .strip().upper() == "RED"),
        None)


