def _decide_journal_view(tid, stage, *, _iter_events, events_path=None,
                        cross_instance_rows=None, types=None, whole_cross_instance=None) -> dict:
    """The ADMISSION LADDER's journal view, and everything that ladder needs out of it:
    `{"rows": [...], "stage_rows": [...], "decisions": [...]}`.

    It is NOT the verb's whole journal dependency, and saying so was this docstring's own error
    (audit-post finding 3, pass 2): the injected `_recorded_commit_sha` folds the journal too — for
    `subject_revision` at post, and again inside the `fix` evidence axis. What holds the SPEC-0190
    rule-10 bound is therefore the ReadScope at the WIRING SITE (`cli.py#cmd_audit_decide`), not this
    function: inside it both this fold and that one serve from ONE physical read per segment. A view
    that promised to be the only reader would be exactly the per-view remedy rule 10 retires.

    It still exists as its own function for two reasons, neither cosmetic. (1) It names the admission
    ladder's journal dependency in one place — a reader sees what the ladder reads, rather than three
    scans threaded through it. (2) It is
    the DRIVABLE seam the SPEC-0190 journal-reader census requires: the census enumerates the
    function that CONTAINS the `_iter_events` call, and a top-level verb cannot be driven by a
    multiset-identity probe because executing it APPENDS to the journal it was handed. Read-only and
    injected, so the probe drives exactly the production code path.

    The read is segment-aware BY INJECTION: `_iter_events` folds the segment SET
    (`journal.segment_rows`, SPEC-0190 rule 4). This is deliberately not a raw text search of the
    journal path, which would see the live segment alone and answer «no such ceiling row» for a card
    whose audit history has rotated — the exact false-absence class rule 4 exists to end.

    `cross_instance_rows=` WIDENS THE CORPUS TO THE OTHER CHECKOUT (T-12338, SPEC-0204 rule 3 /
    SPEC-0168). The rows the ladder needs are not all in ONE checkout: a dispatched worker's ceiling
    row lives in its `task/T-XXXX` worktree journal while the Controller's `owner_directive` row
    lives on main, so a single-checkout read refuses `below-ceiling` from main and
    `directive-unresolved` from the worktree — BOTH measured on T-12335 (2026-09-10 14:02-14:05Z).
    Rule 3 already promised the READER side of this fold; the writer read one checkout.

    IT IS A DELTA, NOT A REPLACEMENT, and that shape is load-bearing rather than stylistic. The host
    passes ONLY the rows the SPEC-0168 fold found in the OTHER instance and NOT in this one (the
    rule-4 cross-instance set), and this function APPENDS them to the injected single-instance read
    it already takes. Two properties fall out that a wholesale «here is the folded corpus» parameter
    would destroy:

      * THE OWN INSTANCE STAYS FRESH UNDER THE LOCK. `cmd_audit_decide` holds the repo lock across
        the read, the duplicate check and the append precisely so a peer cannot append between them
        (rule 4). But the SPEC-0190 rule-10 ReadScope is a PROCESS-GLOBAL memo installed at the
        WIRING SITE, i.e. OUTSIDE that lock and re-entrantly shared — so a corpus folded through it
        may be a snapshot taken before the lock was taken, and the duplicate check would read a
        journal a peer has already moved. Routing the own instance through the injected
        `_iter_events`, inside the lock, as it always was, keeps the critical section's promise
        intact; the foreign instance is a checkout THIS process never appends to, so its staleness
        is not what the lock protects.
      * THE DEDUPE IDENTITY IS NOT RE-IMPLEMENTED. The delta is computed by the fold itself, with the
        write side's own `_event_dedup_key` (rule 3) — so a union-merged row present in BOTH
        instances arrives exactly once, and this module never grows a second identity function.

    `None` (an unwired host, and every existing in-process test) is EXACTLY today's behaviour.

    `types=` (T-13364) DECLARES the event types the caller reads (`DECIDE_ROW_TYPES` for `audit decide`):
    the own read becomes `_iter_events(events_path, types=types)` — served by the verb's indexed scope —
    and the cross rows are cut to the same types, so `rows` is exactly today's rows of those types in
    the same order. None (every other caller) is the untyped read, unchanged. A typed view also
    carries `whole`: a thunk reading the UNTYPED corpus (this checkout + `whole_cross_instance()`, the
    untyped cross rows) for the caller's any-type question — read only when called."""
    if types is None:
        rows = list(_iter_events(events_path)) + list(cross_instance_rows or ())
        whole = None
    else:
        types = frozenset(types)
        rows = list(_iter_events(events_path, types=types)) + [
            r for r in cross_instance_rows or () if isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("type") in types]

        def whole():
            return list(_iter_events(events_path)) + list(
                whole_cross_instance() if whole_cross_instance else ())
    return {"rows": rows,
            "stage_rows": _stage_audit_rows(rows, tid, stage),
            "decisions": _ceiling_decision_rows(rows, tid),
            **({"whole": whole} if whole is not None else {})}


