def _ship_contained_record_only(tid: str, sha: str, *, _bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths,
                               _prior_audit_record, _git_resolve_sha, REPO_ROOT,
                               _card_record_cause=None,
                               _task_commit_landed_chain=None) -> "str | None":
    """T-12614 (SPEC-0204 rules 2-3) — is `sha` a RECORD-ONLY revision that provably CONTAINS this
    card's audited ship? Returns THE SHIP's resolved sha when it is, else None.

    THE CLASS, measured independently by two consumers. A `fix` disposition whose only honest
    artifact is a BOOKKEEPING or JOURNAL commit — which is what every card-record repair and every
    journal-row fix is — had no admissible `evidence_revision`: `empty_audit_subject_refusal`
    (T-11405) refuses such a revision as an audit subject, and `audit decide` resolves `--evidence`
    through that same judgement (T-12542). The residual then had to be recorded `accept`, a
    disposition documented as TERMINAL and meaning the residual STANDS — so the ledger said the
    OPPOSITE of what happened (<project> T-0574 / X-1424, a card-record repair; <project> T-0568 /
    X-1428, journal-row fixes).

    WHY THIS IS NOT A RELAXATION OF T-11405. That guard fences ONE harm: the auditor receives a diff
    with none of the card's work in it. This predicate does not ask to be excused from it — it
    NAMES THE SHIP, and its callers audit THAT. The record-only revision is what the decision
    RECORDS as carrying the change (SPEC-0204 rule 2's own words, which nowhere require that
    revision's own diff to be authored content); the ship is what the auditor is SHOWN. Separating
    the two is the whole change.

    RETURNING THE SHIP RATHER THAN A BOOL is deliberate: both seams — `audit decide`'s write-time
    ladder and the bounded pass's subject resolution — need that value, and deriving it twice is
    precisely how the two could come to disagree (the X-1251 two-doors class). A THIRD reader joined
    on the same terms (T-12715, <project> X-1482): `task close` asks it whether the RECORDED
    commit — a `--fix-red --card-repair` repair, kind-less by design — is a record-only revision
    containing the ship the audit-post record covers, and closes ON THAT SHIP when it is. Without
    that the redirect pinned the ceiling pass's GREEN to the ship while closure demanded the record,
    and the only exit was a further pass the engaged ceiling refuses.

    FOUR CONJUNCTS, each fail-closed, every reader REUSED — no new flag and no new journal consumer,
    so the SPEC-0190 census is untouched (CHARTER §P1 F1/F2):
      (1) `sha`'s OWN diff carries no authored path, through the EXISTING allow-list reader. `[]` is
          record-only; `None` means git could not answer and is NOT `[]`, so an unverifiable
          revision refuses and an uninjected reader refuses. An AUTHORED evidence revision never
          enters this route at all, so today's behaviour for it is byte-identical.
      (2) THE SHIP IS NAMED BY A GOVERNED RECORD, never by the actor: the prior audit-post record
          names the commit it covered. The same source T-11991 conjunct (c) reads, and deliberately
          NOT a `commit_landed` row — a journal row is hand-emittable, and every conjunct here must
          be read from something the actor being fenced does not author.
      (3) THAT SHIP CARRIES AUTHORED CONTENT — T-11991 conjunct (d) verbatim. Without it the pass
          would audit an empty subject, which is the very harm T-11405 exists to prevent; this is
          what makes the redirect honest rather than merely permitted.
      (4) `sha` REACHES that ship along a TRANSPARENT first-parent chain, STRICTLY (`sha` is not the
          ship — «already in the audited commit» stays an `accept`, rule 2). The walk is
          `_card_repair_chain_walk`, shared VERBATIM with T-11991 conjunct (e). This is the conjunct
          that keeps the admission from being a bare «descendant + ship exists»: an AUTHORED commit
          on the chain STOPS the walk, so a record-only commit made anywhere later on the branch is
          REFUSED and unaudited content can never sit between the graded ship and the evidence.

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT REQUIRED, vs `_card_repair_subject_admitted`: the
    `card_repair`/`absorption` MARKER, the «subject's diff carries the CARD's own record» SHAPE, and
    «the RED's cause derives to the card record». Those three are what make that predicate a
    CARD-REPAIR door, and they are exactly what the two reported cases fail — X-1424 fails the
    marker conjunct; X-1428's journal-row fix fails the shape AND the cause conjuncts. They are not
    load-bearing HERE, because the subject the auditor receives is the SHIP by construction rather
    than by permission. The residual risk — a `fix` recorded on a bookkeeping commit where nothing
    was actually fixed — is caught LOUDLY at the seam that judges claims: the auditor re-raises the
    residual as still open and rule 3 makes that RED terminal.

    T-12926 — THE CARD-FIELD FALLBACK FOR CONJUNCT (4), opt-in by its two injections and inert
    without them. Measured on T-12826 (2026-09-24): a ceiling residual located at the card's own
    `implementation_plan`, fixed by amending that field, left a record-only evidence revision that
    reaches the ship only THROUGH `main` — the ship had landed and the card was re-claimed, so
    other cards' authored, separately-audited commits sit on the first-parent chain and the walk
    stops. `audit decide --disposition fix` then refused (T-11405) and `--repin-ship` refused at the
    ceiling, and only an owner `accept` escaped. When the walk fails, (4) is instead satisfied by
    ALL of:
      (4a) the governed audit-post record's RED cause IS the card's own record
           (`_card_record_cause`: the HOST wrapper `cli._red_cause_is_card_record(tid, prior)` —
           TID FIRST, it loads the card itself — over the ONE shared predicate
           `audit_ceiling._red_cause_is_card_record(prior, tid, card=...)`; strand (iv) names a
           card FIELD. Deliberately NOT named after either, so it cannot be confused with this
           module's own `(prior, tid)` re-export),
           so the fix provably lives in the card text the packet renders, not in any diff;
      (4b) `sha` STRICTLY descends from the covered commit (`_git_strict_descendant`);
      (4c) every `commit_landed` of THIS card after the covered commit is provably record-only —
           the card authored nothing of its own since the graded ship (its own later ship refuses).
    What (4c) cannot see, a raw-git commit with no journal row, is still caught at `land` by the
    audited-footprint check (`land_audited_footprint.own_post_audit`). The T-11405 guard is not
    widened: the subject shown is still the authored ship, never the record-only revision.

    Pure; NEVER raises; None on absence, on malformed input, on an uninjected reader and on ANY git
    failure — the posture both sibling predicates hold: a discriminator that cannot verify must not
    grant."""
    try:
        if not callable(_bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths) or not callable(_git_resolve_sha):
            return None
        subject = _git_resolve_sha(str(sha or "")) or ""
        if not subject:
            return None
        # (1) record-only — `[]` exactly; `None` (git could not answer) is not `[]`.
        if _bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths(subject, tid) != []:
            return None
        # (2) the ship, from the GOVERNED audit-post record.
        prior = _prior_audit_record(tid, "post") if callable(_prior_audit_record) else None
        covered = str((prior or {}).get("commit") or "").strip()
        if not covered:
            return None
        ship = _git_resolve_sha(covered) or ""
        if not ship or ship == subject:      # (4) STRICT — the ship itself is an `accept`, not a `fix`
            return None
        # (3) the ship carries authored content, so the range the packet sends is not empty.
        #     T-12829: when the record covers an EARLIER card-repair (record-only), the authored ship
        #     is resolved back through the records each repair folded — the SHARED resolver the two
        #     card-repair doors read — and THAT is the ship returned (what the auditor is shown).
        ship = _card_repair_authored_ship(tid, ship,
                                          _bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths=_bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths,
                                          _git_resolve_sha=_git_resolve_sha, REPO_ROOT=REPO_ROOT)
        if not ship:
            return None
        # (4) transparent first-parent reach — the SHARED walk, so this door and the card-repair
        #     door can never disagree about which crossings are transparent.
        if not _card_repair_chain_walk(subject, covered, tid,
                                       _bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths=_bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths,
                                       _git_resolve_sha=_git_resolve_sha, REPO_ROOT=REPO_ROOT):
            if not _card_field_reach(tid, subject, covered, prior,
                                     _bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths=_bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths,
                                     _git_resolve_sha=_git_resolve_sha, REPO_ROOT=REPO_ROOT,
                                     _card_record_cause=_card_record_cause,
                                     _task_commit_landed_chain=_task_commit_landed_chain):
                return None
        return ship
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001 — an unprovable admission is simply not granted
        return None


def _card_field_reach(tid: str, subject: str, covered: str, prior, *,
                      _bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths, _git_resolve_sha, REPO_ROOT,
                      _card_record_cause, _task_commit_landed_chain) -> bool:
    """T-12926 — conjunct (4)'s card-field fallback, (4a)-(4c) of `_ship_contained_record_only`.
    `_card_record_cause(tid, prior) -> bool` is the HOST wrapper `cli._red_cause_is_card_record`
    (TID FIRST; it loads the card and injects the allow-list) — the same callable `cmd_audit` already
    hands `_require_nonempty_audit_subject`. Pure; never raises; False on anything unprovable or
    uninjected."""
    try:
        if not callable(_card_record_cause) or not callable(_task_commit_landed_chain):
            return False
        # (4a) the recorded RED's cause is the card's own record.
        if _card_record_cause(tid, prior) is not True:
            return False
        # (4b) strict ancestry, the module's ONE notion of descent.
        cov = _git_resolve_sha(str(covered or "")) or ""
        if not cov or _git_strict_descendant(cov, subject, repo_root=REPO_ROOT) is not True:
            return False
        # (4c) nothing of this card's own after the covered commit carries authored content.
        chain = list(_task_commit_landed_chain(tid) or ())
        resolved = [(_git_resolve_sha(str(c or "")) or "") for c, _k in chain]
        if cov not in resolved:
            return False
        after = resolved[len(resolved) - 1 - resolved[::-1].index(cov) + 1:]
        return all(r and _bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths(r, tid) == [] for r in after)
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001 — an unprovable admission is simply not granted
        return False


def _card_repair_rides_audited_ship(tid: str, sha: str, *, _bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths,
                                    _prior_audit_record, _recorded_commit_landed=None,
                                    _custody_commit_landed=None, _git_resolve_sha=None,
                                    REPO_ROOT=None) -> bool:
    """T-12892 — is `sha` a governed `--fix-red --card-repair` commit riding record-only on this
    card's audited ship? The pass-1 sibling of the T-11991 admission, WITHOUT its conjunct (c).

    WHY (c) IS DROPPED HERE: the commit arm derived the RED's cause itself (SPEC-0015 §Internal (C))
    before writing the `card_repair` marker; re-deriving it at the audit door let the two doors
    disagree about one RED (T-12826: `4.00/20.00` in an evidence-gap finding lexed as an authored
    path), so the repair had no executable audit route. What still carries the admission is read from
    git and the governed audit-post record, never from the actor — `_ship_contained_record_only`:
    the subject is record-only, the record names an AUTHORED ship, and the subject reaches it along a
    TRANSPARENT first-parent chain. The auditor then receives the cumulative range, ship included.

    Two conjuncts, both fail-closed:
      (1) the subject IS the custody commit (custody view; raw row when none is injected) and that
          row carries `card_repair: true` — an unmarked bookkeeping commit is refused as before;
      (2) `_ship_contained_record_only` names a ship.
    Pure; never raises; False on anything unprovable."""
    try:
        read = _custody_commit_landed or _recorded_commit_landed
        rec = read(tid) if callable(read) else None
        if not isinstance(rec, dict) or rec.get("card_repair") is not True:
            return False
        if not callable(_git_resolve_sha):
            return False
        subject = _git_resolve_sha(str(sha or "")) or ""
        if not subject or (_git_resolve_sha(str(rec.get("commit") or "").strip()) or "") != subject:
            return False
        return bool(_ship_contained_record_only(
            tid, subject, _bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths=_bookkeeping_commit_authored_paths,
            _prior_audit_record=_prior_audit_record, _git_resolve_sha=_git_resolve_sha,
            REPO_ROOT=REPO_ROOT))
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001 — an unprovable admission is simply not granted
        return False


@functools.wraps(audit_packet._card_repair_subject_admitted)
def _card_repair_subject_admitted(*a, **kw):
    """T-12701 host residue — the body now lives in `bin/lib/audit_packet.py#_card_repair_subject_admitted`."""
    _packet_inject(kw, "_card_repair_subject_admitted")
    return audit_packet._card_repair_subject_admitted(*a, **kw)


# ── T-12026 (X-1251, <project> T-0603) — THE DERIVED CARD-RECORD CAUSE ────────────────────────────
# The path/extension lexer and the acceptance-criterion lexer below are LEXERS, not vocabularies:
# they only say "this token has the SHAPE of a repo path / of an AC id". WHICH of the tokens they
# find counts is decided entirely by the injected T-11991 allow-list and by the CARD'S OWN
# acceptance — never by a word this module knows. That distinction is the whole point of this
# card: the retired predicate asked what the auditor WROTE, this one asks what the record REFERS TO.
# The lexer takes ANY slash-joined token, with or without a file extension — `bin/yitc-v2`, this
# repo's own CLI, is EXTENSIONLESS, and requiring a dot made the single most-authored path in the
# corpus invisible to part (1) (audit-post pass 1). Trailing `#symbol` / `:line` locators are
# stripped before classification, since an auditor writes `bin/yitc-v2#_emit_cli_invoked` and
# `bin/lib/task.py:100` far more often than a bare path.
_RED_CAUSE_PATH_RE = audit_ceiling._RED_CAUSE_PATH_RE  # T-12706: moved module-local to bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py (re-export alias)
_RED_CAUSE_LOCATOR_RE = audit_ceiling._RED_CAUSE_LOCATOR_RE  # T-12706: moved module-local to bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py (re-export alias)
_RED_CAUSE_AC_RE = re.compile(r"\bAC-?(\d{1,3})\b", re.IGNORECASE)
_ACCEPTANCE_AC_RE = audit_ceiling._ACCEPTANCE_AC_RE  # T-12706: moved module-local to bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py (re-export alias)

# T-12616 (X-1426, <project> T-0582) — the SECOND schema coordinate `_red_cause_is_card_record` reads
# BY NAME, beside `criterion_ref`. `failing_input` is the finding's REPRO INPUT — SPEC-0036 §Saved
# audit result and SPEC-0200 rule 3(a) both define it as «the concrete input/state that fails», i.e.
# what you RUN to see the defect. It is never a claim about WHERE the fix lands; the fields that say
# that are `where` / `locator` / `fix`, and they are read unchanged.
#
# WHY READING A KEY HERE IS NOT THE RETIRED KEYWORD TEST, which is the one real objection to it. The
# predicate reads every VALUE precisely so it needs no per-auditor KEY vocabulary — «that would just
# be the retired keyword test moved from the values to the keys». That objection does not reach this
# key for the same reason it does not reach `criterion_ref`: both are members of
# `AUDITOR_SUPPLIED_FINDING_FIELDS`, the response contract SPEC-0036's saved-record schema itself
# declares, so the meaning is the SCHEMA'S and not a guess about how this auditor phrased things. The
# name is DERIVED from that tuple rather than spelled loose, so a schema rename cannot leave a stale
# literal behind granting a skip for a key the contract no longer defines (it fails closed to `None`,
# which matches no key, so the skip simply stops firing).
_RED_CAUSE_REPRO_FIELD = next(
    (k for k in AUDITOR_SUPPLIED_FINDING_FIELDS if k == "failing_input"), None)

# T-12693 (successor of T-12672; incident T-12663) — the THIRD schema coordinate the predicate reads
# BY NAME: `fix`, the finding's REMEDY (SPEC-0036 §Saved audit result: `fix: <one-line concrete
# fix>`, beside `where: <file:line OR description>` — the schema puts the defect's LOCATION in
# `where` / `locator` and the REMEDY in `fix`). Derived from `LEGACY_FINDING_FIELDS` for the same
# reason `_RED_CAUSE_REPRO_FIELD` is derived from its tuple: a schema rename fails closed to `None`,
# which matches no key, so the skip below simply stops firing rather than leaving a stale literal.
_RED_CAUSE_FIX_FIELD = next((k for k in LEGACY_FINDING_FIELDS if k == "fix"), None)
# The two LOCATING coordinates, read for condition (iii) of the same skip ONLY — the part-(1)
# refusal loop reads them byte-unchanged like every other value.
_RED_CAUSE_LOCATING_FIELDS = tuple(
    k for k in LEGACY_FINDING_FIELDS + AUDITOR_SUPPLIED_FINDING_FIELDS if k in ("where", "locator"))
# The FIXED literal edit-verb tuple — the edit-instruction shape T-12672's auditor named
# (fp1:1eab54a24173413c: «… then edit `<subject>` so the assertion …»). Matched as STEMS
# (`editing`, `changed`, `fixture` all hit) — a false hit only over-refuses, the safe direction for
# a helper that GRANTS. The tuple is closed by design; a verb outside it is caught by the POSITIVE
# test below, not by growing the list.
_RED_CAUSE_EDIT_VERBS = ("edit", "change", "modify", "rewrite", "patch", "update", "add",
                         "remove", "delete", "replace", "rename", "fix")
_RED_CAUSE_EDIT_VERB_RE = re.compile(r"\b(?:" + "|".join(_RED_CAUSE_EDIT_VERBS) + r")",
                                     re.IGNORECASE)
# The POSITIVE structural proof (audit-pre attempt 1 on T-12693: «a denylist does not PROVE the
# later quoted subject carries no edit instruction»): the subject span must be the immediate OBJECT
# of one of these prepositions («… showing no entry for `<subject>`»), never of a verb («… then
# edit `<subject>`», «… then adjust `<subject>`» — no preposition precedes the subject, so neither
# is exempt whether or not its verb is in the tuple above).
_RED_CAUSE_OBJECT_PREPOSITIONS = ("for", "of", "in", "on", "at", "from", "against", "over",
                                  "about", "under")
_RED_CAUSE_BETWEEN_MAX_WORDS = 12
_RED_CAUSE_QUOTED_SPAN_RE = re.compile(r"`([^`\n]+)`|'([^'\n]+)'|\"([^\"\n]+)\"")


def _quoted_spans(value: str) -> list:
    """T-12693 — every backtick / single / double quoted span of `value`, as (start, end, inner)
    with `start`/`end` the offsets of the INNER text. Pure lexing; `[]` on a non-string."""
    if not isinstance(value, str):
        return []
    out = []
    for m in _RED_CAUSE_QUOTED_SPAN_RE.finditer(value):
        inner = m.group(1) or m.group(2) or m.group(3) or ""
        if inner.strip():
            out.append((m.start() + 1, m.end() - 1, inner))
    return out


def _repo_root_dir(REPO_ROOT):
    """The readable REPO_ROOT as a Path, else None — the granting-side root check every T-12693
    helper shares (`lessons/carving-an-exception-into-a-fail-closed-gate` §1: a helper that GRANTS
    yields nothing on an unreadable root, the OPPOSITE polarity to `_repo_path_tokens`)."""
    try:
        if REPO_ROOT is None:
            return None
        cand = Path(str(REPO_ROOT))
        return cand if cand.is_dir() else None
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001 — an unreadable root grants nothing
        return None


def _quoted_invocation_spans(value: str, REPO_ROOT=None) -> list:
    """T-12693 — the quoted spans of `value` that are VERB INVOCATIONS: the span's FIRST
    whitespace-word lexes via `_repo_path_tokens` to a token that is an EXECUTABLE FILE at
    REPO_ROOT (`is_file()` + `X_OK` — derived from the repo, never from a list of verb or binary
    names) and the span carries at least one FURTHER word (`bin/yitc-v2 task test --run`; a bare
    `bin/yitc-v2` is a path, not an invocation). Returns [(start, end, path_token)] in order.
    `[]` on an unreadable REPO_ROOT — this helper feeds a skip that GRANTS."""
    root = _repo_root_dir(REPO_ROOT)
    if root is None:
        return []
    out = []
    for start, end, inner in _quoted_spans(value):
        words = inner.split()
        if len(words) < 2:
            continue
        toks = _repo_path_tokens(words[0], REPO_ROOT)
        if len(toks) != 1:
            continue
        tok = next(iter(toks))
        try:
            target = root / tok
            if target.is_file() and os.access(str(target), os.X_OK):
                out.append((start, end, tok))
        except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001 — an unanswerable check is not an invocation
            continue
    return out


def _card_subject_paths(card, REPO_ROOT=None) -> set:
    """T-12693 — the card's OWN SUBJECT files: the repo-path tokens of its STRUCTURED
    `expected_touch` forecast ONLY (each entry a validated in-repo file or glob — `task analyze
    --expected-touch`, X-0440 / T-11251 — refreshed by `task commit` from the committed diff). NEVER
    `scope`, `title`, `analysis` or any prose field: T-12693's audit-pre pass 1
    (fp1:d59b403d280c0e2f) showed that lexing scope prose makes every path the card merely MENTIONS
    a subject. Read through the ONE `_repo_path_tokens` lexer, so no second path vocabulary is
    born. `set()` on a malformed card or an unreadable REPO_ROOT (granting polarity)."""
    if not isinstance(card, dict) or _repo_root_dir(REPO_ROOT) is None:
        return set()
    touch = card.get("expected_touch") or []
    if isinstance(touch, str):
        touch = [touch]
    out: set = set()
    try:
        for entry in touch:
            out |= _repo_path_tokens(str(entry or ""), REPO_ROOT)
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001 — an unreadable forecast declares no subject
        return set()
    return out


def _subject_follows_cited_probe(value: str, anchor_end: int, subject_start: int, finding: dict,
                                 cited_acs: set, tid: str, subjects: set, REPO_ROOT=None) -> bool:
    """T-12693 — the CONTEXT test that proves a card-subject token quoted AFTER the cited probe is
    the probe's OBJECT rather than an edit target. Replaces T-12672's quoted-object-FOLLOWING arm,
    which exempted on quote ORDERING alone and was refused at audit-pre terminal RED
    (fp1:1eab54a24173413c: «citation and quote ordering do not prove that the subject is merely
    bookkeeping evidence»). ALL of the following must hold — any one failing → not exempt → the
    part-(1) refusal stands:

      (ii)   the prose BETWEEN the anchor span and the subject span carries no repo-path token and
             no `_RED_CAUSE_EDIT_VERBS` stem — the edit-instruction shape the auditor named;
      (ii-b) the POSITIVE proof: that prose is at most `_RED_CAUSE_BETWEEN_MAX_WORDS` words and its
             LAST word is one of `_RED_CAUSE_OBJECT_PREPOSITIONS`, so the subject is read as the
             object of a preposition («… showing no entry for `<subject>`»), never of a verb;
      (iii)  the finding's `locator`, read UNCHANGED, is the auditor's OWN structured statement
             that the defect lives in the CARD RECORD: it lexes to this card's own
             `tasks/<tid>-*.yaml` path AND carries an AC id the finding cites (the T-12663 shape,
             `tasks/T-12663-….yaml:acceptance[AC3]` beside `criterion_ref: AC3`) — SPEC-0036 defines
             `locator` as WHERE the defect is, so an auditor who locates it AT the card's criterion
             and in the same finding instructs an edit of the subject would contradict their own
             coordinate; and neither `where` nor `locator` lexes to a card-subject token.

    Condition (i) — the anchor is the cited criterion's OWN probe — is checked by the caller, which
    only calls this for an anchor whose path token is in the T-12433 `named` set. Pure; `False` on
    any unprovable input (this helper GRANTS). Kept as its own function so the AC2 differential can
    stub it to always-True and prove the survivor fixture is then admitted — the test has teeth."""
    try:
        if not isinstance(value, str) or not isinstance(finding, dict) or not subjects:
            return False
        if anchor_end < 0 or subject_start <= anchor_end:
            return False
        between = value[anchor_end:subject_start]
        # strip the closing quote of the anchor and the opening quote of the subject
        between = between.strip("`'\"")
        if _repo_path_tokens(between, REPO_ROOT):
            return False                                          # (ii) a path between the spans
        if _RED_CAUSE_EDIT_VERB_RE.search(between):
            return False                                          # (ii) an edit instruction
        words = between.split()
        if not words or len(words) > _RED_CAUSE_BETWEEN_MAX_WORDS:
            return False                                          # (ii-b) unbounded prose
        last = words[-1].strip(".,;:()[]{}\"'`").lower()
        if last not in _RED_CAUSE_OBJECT_PREPOSITIONS:
            return False                                          # (ii-b) not a preposition's object
        # the locating coordinates, read THROUGH the schema-derived tuple (a rename that drops a
        # key from the declaration tuples drops it here too — nothing is spelled loose)
        coords = {k: str(finding.get(k) or "") for k in _RED_CAUSE_LOCATING_FIELDS}
        locator = coords.get("locator", "")
        where = coords.get("where", "")
        loc_toks = _repo_path_tokens(locator, REPO_ROOT)
        if not any(_is_own_card_path(t, tid) for t in loc_toks):
            return False                                          # (iii) not located in the card
        loc_acs = {m.group(1).lstrip("0") or "0" for m in _RED_CAUSE_AC_RE.finditer(locator)}
        if not (loc_acs & set(cited_acs or ())):
            return False                                          # (iii) not AT the cited criterion
        if (loc_toks | _repo_path_tokens(where, REPO_ROOT)) & subjects:
            return False                                          # (iii) locates at the subject
        return True
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001 — an unprovable context grants nothing
        return False


# The AFFIRMATIVE probe-request markers (T-12693 audit-post pass 2, fp1:31882fe70718ec90 — «a
# denylist over a truncated clause is not positive proof»; NARROWED by T-12712 after the T-12693
# on-decisions pass, fp1:3e2322be835f8807 — «a bare preposition is not proof the command is RUN»):
# the phrase IMMEDIATELY before a quoted invocation must DENOTE EXECUTION for the invocation to
# read as REQUESTED FOR EXECUTION. Three FIXED tuples, like the edit-verb tuple:
#   * `_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_REQUEST_MARKERS` — the run-verb stems, sufficient on their own («run
#     `<probe>`»). No preposition or conjunction is in this tuple any more: `of` / `for` / `and` /
#     `via` / `from` / `with` let «Tamper with `<probe>` to suppress the entry.» (`tamper` is outside
#     the edit-verb denylist, by design — the denylist is a fence, not the proof) read as a request.
#   * `_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_REQUEST_VERBS` — the evidence-request verbs («record», «capture») that GOVERN
#   * `_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_EVIDENCE_HEADS` — the evidence-noun heads («the output of», «results for»),
#     which are NEVER sufficient alone (T-12712 audit-pre pass 1, fp1:028030e7a04099f3: «Tamper with
#     the result of `<probe>`» ends in a head and proves nothing).
# A preposition counts ONLY as part of one of those heads, and `and` ONLY as the tail of a bounded,
# punctuation-free `both … and` COORDINATION under such a governing phrase (the T-12663 finding-1
# shape «Record plausible Stage-6 results for both the direct-file run and `<probe>`»).
_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_REQUEST_MARKERS = ("run", "runs", "running", "re-run", "rerun", "execute",
                                    "executes", "executing", "invoke", "invokes", "invoking")
_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_REQUEST_VERBS = ("record", "records", "recording", "re-record", "capture",
                                  "captures", "capturing")
_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_EVIDENCE_HEADS = ("output of", "output for", "result of", "results of",
                                   "results for")
# A GAP / COORDINATION word: no punctuation, no quote/backtick (a quoted span inside the run would
# be a DIFFERENT invocation), and never a conjunction that could coordinate a second VERB («Record
# and tamper with the result of …»).
_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_GAP_WORD = r"(?!(?:and|then|but|nor)\s)[^\s,;:.!?`'\"()\[\]{}]+"
_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_GAP_MAX_WORDS = 6


def _build_probe_request_marker_re(markers=None, verbs=None, heads=None):
    """T-12712 — the anchored, case-insensitive, WHOLE-WORD read of the probe-request grammar at the
    END of the prefix before a quoted invocation:
        EXEC_VERB [COORD]  |  REQUEST_VERB GAP{0,6} EVIDENCE_HEAD [COORD]
    COORD = `both GAP{0,6} and` — `both` is REQUIRED: without it the run-verb stem read as a NOUN
    («Tamper with results for both the direct-file run and `<probe>`» ends in `run and`) would
    satisfy the exec arm, the very shape T-12712's audit-pre named. Built once at import from the three fixed tuples; the
    parameters exist ONLY so a differential test can rebuild it with the retired T-12693 tuple and
    show the tamper fixture wrongly passing — production code never passes them."""
    def alt(items):
        return "(?:" + "|".join(re.escape(x) for x in sorted(items, key=len, reverse=True)) + ")"
    gap = r"(?:" + _RED_CAUSE_PROBE_GAP_WORD + r"\s+){0,%d}" % _RED_CAUSE_PROBE_GAP_MAX_WORDS
    coord = r"(?:\s+both\s+" + gap + r"and)?"
    exec_arm = alt(markers or _RED_CAUSE_PROBE_REQUEST_MARKERS) + coord
    request_arm = (alt(verbs or _RED_CAUSE_PROBE_REQUEST_VERBS) + r"\s+" + gap
                   + alt(heads or _RED_CAUSE_PROBE_EVIDENCE_HEADS) + coord)
    return re.compile(r"(?<![\w-])(?:" + exec_arm + "|" + request_arm + r")[\s.,;:()\[\]{}\"'`]*$",
                      re.IGNORECASE)


_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_REQUEST_MARKER_RE = _build_probe_request_marker_re()


def _invocation_requested_as_probe(value: str, span_start: int) -> bool:
    """T-12693 (audit-post pass 1 fp1:3c1dd42d79f4f12b, pass 2 fp1:31882fe70718ec90) + T-12712
    (fp1:3e2322be835f8807) — is the quoted invocation at `span_start` being REQUESTED AS AN EXECUTED
    PROBE («Record the output of `<probe>`», «… results for both the direct-file run and
    `<probe>`»), rather than named as an EDIT TARGET («Modify `<probe>` to suppress the entry»,
    «Modify the command: `<probe>` …») or merely MENTIONED («Tamper with `<probe>` …», «Tamper with
    the result of `<probe>` …»)? BOTH halves must hold, read from the WHOLE of the value that
    precedes the span — never a clause cut at punctuation, which is what let an edit instruction
    separated from its invocation by a `:` vanish (T-12693 pass 2's failing input):
      * NEGATIVE — no `_RED_CAUSE_EDIT_VERBS` stem anywhere in that prefix;
      * AFFIRMATIVE — the prefix ENDS in the execution-denoting grammar
        `_RED_CAUSE_PROBE_REQUEST_MARKER_RE` reads as a WHOLE word/phrase (whitespace and closing
        punctuation skipped, never a substring: `pre-run` / `running` are not `run`): a run-verb
        stem, or an evidence-request verb governing an evidence-noun head, either optionally
        carrying a bounded coordination tail. A bare preposition or conjunction before the span
        proves nothing (T-12712).
    An invocation that fails either is FENCED: not exempt under arm (a), anchors nothing under (b),
    and `_red_cause_is_card_record` subtracts its path token even from the cited criterion's
    `named` set, because an instruction to edit the probe is an authored-code instruction whoever
    names the probe. Over-refusal («Nothing to edit here. Record the output of `<probe>`» is
    fenced by the prefix-wide denylist; «Run the suite with `<probe>`» is refused) is the safe
    direction for a helper that GRANTS. Pure; `False` on any unprovable input."""
    try:
        if not isinstance(value, str) or span_start < 0:
            return False
        head = value[:span_start]
        if _RED_CAUSE_EDIT_VERB_RE.search(head):
            return False
        return _RED_CAUSE_PROBE_REQUEST_MARKER_RE.search(head) is not None
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001
        return False


def _fix_edit_fenced_invocation_paths(value: str, REPO_ROOT=None) -> set:
    """T-12693 — the path tokens of the quoted invocations in `value` that FAIL
    `_invocation_requested_as_probe`. Subtracted from every exemption of a `fix` value, `named`
    included. `set()` on an unreadable REPO_ROOT: nothing is then lexed as an invocation either, so
    the value's tokens are judged exactly as before this card."""
    try:
        return {tok for s, _e, tok in _quoted_invocation_spans(value, REPO_ROOT)
                if not _invocation_requested_as_probe(value, s)}
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001
        return set()


def _fix_invocation_exempt_paths(value: str, card, named: set, tid: str, finding: dict,
                                 cited_acs: set, REPO_ROOT=None) -> set:
    """T-12693 — the repo-path tokens of a finding's `fix` value that are SKIPPED by
    `_red_cause_is_card_record` part (1) because they are tied to a QUOTED VERB INVOCATION:

      (a)     every invocation's OWN path token — the auditor quoting a command you RUN (T-12663
              finding 1: `bin/yitc-v2 task test --run`, no criterion_ref) — PROVIDED the clause
              before it requests it as an executed probe (`_invocation_requested_as_probe`; an
              invocation named as an EDIT TARGET, «Modify `<probe>` …», is fenced and exempts and
              anchors nothing — audit-post pass 1, fp1:3c1dd42d79f4f12b);
      (b-ARG) a card-subject token (`_card_subject_paths`) inside the SAME quoted span as an
              invocation the CITED criterion names verbatim (its path token is in `named`);
      (b-CTX) a card-subject token inside a LATER quoted span of the same value, anchored on such
              a cited-probe invocation, ONLY when `_subject_follows_cited_probe` proves the context.

    With no invocation in the value NOTHING is skipped (T-12672's pass-1 failing input — `fix:
    Change `<subject>` to correct the predicate` — refuses as before). An unquoted subject, one
    quoted BEFORE the anchor, or one beside an invocation no cited criterion names, is not exempt.
    SKIPPED, never admitted: the result satisfies no strand of part (2). `set()` on any unprovable
    input or an unreadable REPO_ROOT (granting polarity)."""
    try:
        invocations = [inv for inv in _quoted_invocation_spans(value, REPO_ROOT)
                       if _invocation_requested_as_probe(value, inv[0])]
        if not invocations:
            return set()
        out = {tok for _s, _e, tok in invocations}                                   # (a)
        subjects = _card_subject_paths(card, REPO_ROOT)
        if not subjects:
            return out
        spans = _quoted_spans(value)
        for a_start, a_end, a_tok in invocations:
            if a_tok not in (named or set()):
                continue                                          # (i) not the cited probe
            inner = value[a_start:a_end]
            out |= _repo_path_tokens(inner, REPO_ROOT) & subjects                     # (b-ARG)
            for s_start, s_end, s_inner in spans:
                if s_start <= a_end:
                    continue                                      # same span or BEFORE the anchor
                hit = _repo_path_tokens(s_inner, REPO_ROOT) & subjects
                if hit and _subject_follows_cited_probe(value, a_end, s_start, finding, cited_acs,
                                                        tid, subjects, REPO_ROOT):
                    out |= hit                                                        # (b-CTX)
        return out
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001 — an unprovable skip is simply not granted
        return set()


_repo_path_tokens = audit_ceiling._repo_path_tokens  # T-12706 host residue — the body now lives in `bin/lib/audit_ceiling.py#_repo_path_tokens` (inject-free: re-export alias)


