# ── SPEC-0200 rules 8-9 — the INERT instrumentation (T-12181, plan card C4) ──────────────────────
#
# WHAT THIS BLOCK IS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT. Rules 8-9 name four measurements the plan's
# postcheck needs: the escape ACCOUNTING with its deduped first-discovery deviation, the idempotent
# SUCCESSOR filing, the eleven durable EPISODE fields, and the FOLD that reads them. Everything below
# is CALLABLE and TESTED and is WIRED INTO NOTHING LIVE: no prompt changes, no predicate changes, no
# emit site calls any of it. After this card lands a live `audit consult` / auto-consult emits an
# `external_audit_completed` row carrying NONE of the eleven fields — the legacy row contract is
# unchanged (rule 9's B11). The FIRST live emission is card C3's (T-12182), which wires these
# helpers into `_consult_adjudicate` together with the prompt and the predicates, because rule 7 says
# a partially-landed state is not a valid state of this contract.
#
# WHY THE HELPERS ARE PURE f(delta, rows) WITH THE READER INJECTED. The C2 core already returns the
# whole record delta; every quantity rule 8 counts is DERIVABLE from it, so nothing here reaches back
# into C2 (whose own inertness pin must keep passing) and nothing here opens a file of its own. The
# ONE reader that matters — the journal — arrives INJECTED, and that injection is the correctness
# property, not a style: the host passes `cli._iter_events`, which routes through
# `journal.segment_rows` (SPEC-0190 rule 4), so a dedupe survives ROTATION. A helper that read the
# live segment itself would re-emit every deviation the moment an archive segment appeared, which is
# exactly the failing input this card's AC1 names.

#: Rule 8 — the ONE fingerprint every baseline-completeness escape deviation carries.
